WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:16.379
ENGELBART: I had just left
it set up, the laptop,

00:00:16.379 --> 00:00:18.180
and the projector was on too.

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:20.850
Then I was sitting there
and I noticed that I had not

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:21.990
plugged in any power.

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:24.090
So my batteries would
probably run out halfway

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:25.180
through the presentation.

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:28.140
So when I came up here to
find a way to plug it in,

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:30.210
I inadvertently unplugged that.

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:35.220
And so this would
make a great start

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:39.370
to an interesting
environment between us here.

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:42.097
And I'm going to
have to reboot and--

00:00:42.097 --> 00:00:43.430
AUDIENCE: It's working now, sir.

00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:44.180
ENGELBART: Pardon?

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:45.030
Oh?

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:47.130
Oh!

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:48.644
Thank you very much.

00:00:48.644 --> 00:00:49.810
That's the story of my life.

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:00.060
Okay, we'll be in
there in a minute.

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:02.430
One of the one of
the things that

00:01:02.430 --> 00:01:06.780
caught my ear where Tom
Ferguson talking about

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:13.890
is the basic shift in
paradigms that occurred

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:17.820
in the launching of LAI, about
the way in which organizations

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:20.520
work and what you do your
productivity, et cetera.

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:26.490
And this is something
that, by the early '60s,

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:32.100
I had realized was a very big
plus, that the paradigms that

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:35.400
prevail with which to sort
of guide the way people

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:40.650
look at the world just
have a surprising impact.

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:42.630
And they've always been there.

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:46.320
And there's a slow,
basic change in paradigms

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:51.340
that's sort of part of the
evolution of a culture.

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:55.200
And it's very likely
that it had a survival

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:57.240
value in having
that be relatively

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:02.250
slow, so a segment of society
didn't shift all of its beliefs

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:05.530
and orientations too
rapidly and fall apart.

00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:06.910
So perhaps that's the case.

00:02:06.910 --> 00:02:11.370
But it does take a long
time for paradigms to shift.

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:16.410
Well, one of the things I really
am coming to in this situation

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:20.220
here is looking at
the scale of change

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:23.310
that the technology is
catapulting us into,

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:28.230
and that all of our paradigms
are sort of very slowly

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:32.340
emerging from the old days in
which things changed relatively

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:33.510
slowly.

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:37.560
So how we and social bodies--

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:42.350
organizations, institutions--
how we cope with the future,

00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:46.290
you know, the real perspective
of explosive changes

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:51.780
in technologies, is
sort of out of date.

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:53.610
And that's a very basic thing.

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:56.550
And I learned it in spades
by being fired and things

00:02:56.550 --> 00:02:57.840
like that.

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:03.750
And applying for
universities and corporations

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:08.280
to come and get started in these
things in times in which they

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:11.160
just clearly said, well, no--

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:14.760
one very prominent
computer-oriented industry

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:17.730
actually turned me
down in 1957 when

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:21.000
I said I'm assuming you're
going to go into digital work

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:22.670
with computers.

00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:23.700
No, Doug, sorry.

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:26.550
Not a chance.

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:30.660
So these really, really brought
home to me this question of it.

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:34.650
And so, if there were any kind
of thing that could get across

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:36.720
here from my
perspective, it would

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:39.480
be how do you get
your perspectives

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:42.390
and your paradigms
to start readapting,

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:45.010
really reassess
the way they are?

00:03:45.010 --> 00:03:52.480
So one of them here is in time,
the external environment--

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:56.370
which in this case, a lot of
it is spurred by the digital

00:03:56.370 --> 00:04:00.290
technology and nanotechnologies
that are emerging--

00:04:00.290 --> 00:04:02.470
in which, over and
over and over again,

00:04:02.470 --> 00:04:04.420
we sort of feel
that's come here.

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:06.390
And yes, there are a few
adaptations and such.

00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:09.120
So our job is to take
what the vendors offer

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:10.440
and learn how to adapt it.

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:11.730
It's sort of like that.

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:14.700
But the changes that are
available and the potentials

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:18.779
are just way over that, and
their impact on organizations

00:04:18.779 --> 00:04:24.000
is a really central,
central issue in change.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:28.710
And the organization's evolution
in adapting to that stuff

00:04:28.710 --> 00:04:31.750
is going in these
kind of jerky steps,

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:33.900
including what LAI
has done like that.

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:38.460
And so how do we shift and
cope with that really, really

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:42.200
significant change in there?

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:46.150
And this is just one of
the very basic things.

00:04:46.150 --> 00:04:49.210
And with it come real problems.

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:53.170
So it's just
inevitable that, when

00:04:53.170 --> 00:04:56.650
all that collides throughout the
world in all sorts of aspects

00:04:56.650 --> 00:04:59.320
of its business,
of its economies,

00:04:59.320 --> 00:05:02.140
and the defenses and
all that, that they

00:05:02.140 --> 00:05:10.850
become really strange situations
and very big, very big.

00:05:10.850 --> 00:05:12.232
So that's one thing.

00:05:12.232 --> 00:05:14.440
They said, well, how are we
going to cope with those?

00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:18.790
Well, we cope with those
by collective action

00:05:18.790 --> 00:05:22.180
and thinking about it, adapting
our ways of doing it such,

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:25.220
and finding ways to
solve problems like that.

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:28.840
So there are opportunities
with all of this, too.

00:05:28.840 --> 00:05:30.940
And one of the big,
big opportunities

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:33.370
that just I seized upon--

00:05:33.370 --> 00:05:35.873
you won't believe
it, but it was 1951.

00:05:35.873 --> 00:05:38.230
I was saying computers.

00:05:38.230 --> 00:05:41.142
And we could interact with
them on displays and shifting.

00:05:41.142 --> 00:05:42.850
The way you can couple
your head in brand

00:05:42.850 --> 00:05:45.760
new ways to knowledge, the way
you can collaborate with others

00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:48.400
in there, is just
a huge potential

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:51.310
there for boosting
our capability

00:05:51.310 --> 00:05:54.490
for effective collective
action in there like that.

00:05:54.490 --> 00:05:57.220
Well another thing
that, as a few years

00:05:57.220 --> 00:06:01.060
went beyond that like
that, it's also, say.

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:04.510
We can plug that kind
of new capability back

00:06:04.510 --> 00:06:08.500
into the improvement
infrastructure

00:06:08.500 --> 00:06:12.370
and improve the way
we improve, which,

00:06:12.370 --> 00:06:15.070
with the challenge of how
rapidly things are changing

00:06:15.070 --> 00:06:17.530
and the complex need for
that kind of improvement,

00:06:17.530 --> 00:06:19.240
looked like a sure winner.

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:21.930
And I just used the
term bootstrapping.

00:06:21.930 --> 00:06:25.900
So that was driving me
through those early years.

00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:28.570
And then I ran
into this paradigm

00:06:28.570 --> 00:06:30.910
in the mid '70s
that, on one hand,

00:06:30.910 --> 00:06:33.725
the artificial intelligence
people were saying, oh look.

00:06:33.725 --> 00:06:35.350
The computers are
going to get so smart

00:06:35.350 --> 00:06:37.420
that the humans sitting
in front of them

00:06:37.420 --> 00:06:38.710
won't have to learn anything.

00:06:38.710 --> 00:06:41.150
The computer will adapt to that.

00:06:41.150 --> 00:06:42.790
And on the other
hand, the people

00:06:42.790 --> 00:06:45.274
were talking about
office automation.

00:06:45.274 --> 00:06:46.690
What we're going
to do is automate

00:06:46.690 --> 00:06:48.640
the way we do work now.

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:51.640
And the real user
is the secretary.

00:06:51.640 --> 00:06:56.950
And those two things just pushed
me right off the research map.

00:06:56.950 --> 00:07:00.310
But fortunately, we had
enough doing-- we were already

00:07:00.310 --> 00:07:03.790
supporting, mostly, DOD
units over the ARPANET

00:07:03.790 --> 00:07:05.030
and learning how to use this.

00:07:05.030 --> 00:07:07.090
So we had a small business.

00:07:07.090 --> 00:07:09.124
And we got that to be sold.

00:07:09.124 --> 00:07:10.540
And I went to the
commercial world

00:07:10.540 --> 00:07:14.950
with that, which was
a really good lesson.

00:07:14.950 --> 00:07:17.590
It just clamped
the research down

00:07:17.590 --> 00:07:20.750
because the people who know
how to run businesses says, no.

00:07:20.750 --> 00:07:23.069
You don't get involved
with the customer.

00:07:23.069 --> 00:07:24.110
They'll just want things.

00:07:27.340 --> 00:07:29.800
So then another
very fortunate thing

00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:33.640
is that McDonnell Douglas
with, perhaps, a foolish sort

00:07:33.640 --> 00:07:35.500
of thought or
something like that,

00:07:35.500 --> 00:07:38.530
had been successful in
commercially getting access

00:07:38.530 --> 00:07:40.030
to its CAD systems.

00:07:40.030 --> 00:07:42.160
So it wanted to go into
the information services

00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:43.690
business in a bigger way.

00:07:43.690 --> 00:07:45.200
And it started
acquiring companies,

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:46.700
including the one
that had bought me

00:07:46.700 --> 00:07:48.770
from the research outfit.

00:07:48.770 --> 00:07:50.590
And so bingo.

00:07:50.590 --> 00:07:54.490
I was part of McDonnell Douglas,
which gave me a new freedom

00:07:54.490 --> 00:07:58.490
to start going inside
their design, development,

00:07:58.490 --> 00:08:03.070
manufacturing people without
seeming like a vendor.

00:08:03.070 --> 00:08:04.664
I was a cousin.

00:08:04.664 --> 00:08:05.830
And that was a real thought.

00:08:05.830 --> 00:08:07.510
So it just became
very clear to me

00:08:07.510 --> 00:08:11.710
then that there was just
an immense value, that

00:08:11.710 --> 00:08:14.770
what we were offering in the
way of collaborative work,

00:08:14.770 --> 00:08:19.830
with hypermedia, that we could
do quite a few more things then

00:08:19.830 --> 00:08:23.080
than you yet can do
with the web stuff,

00:08:23.080 --> 00:08:26.960
because it's really based
upon where you can go.

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:30.700
So anyway, these
are the things I'd

00:08:30.700 --> 00:08:34.429
like to talk about in
this sort of order.

00:08:34.429 --> 00:08:38.860
And this was an experiment,
to give you an order that

00:08:38.860 --> 00:08:40.730
wasn't just exactly linear.

00:08:40.730 --> 00:08:43.450
So the first thing to
talk about is what we

00:08:43.450 --> 00:08:46.370
call the co-evolution frontier.

00:08:46.370 --> 00:08:49.000
And it's like
saying, well, where

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:51.670
does organizational
capability come from?

00:08:51.670 --> 00:08:57.080
Well, it's just based upon your
basic genetic capabilities,

00:08:57.080 --> 00:08:57.580
you know?

00:08:57.580 --> 00:09:03.370
You've got sensory, perceptual,
mental, cognitive, motor

00:09:03.370 --> 00:09:04.750
capabilities.

00:09:04.750 --> 00:09:07.660
And these are the
things you exercise.

00:09:07.660 --> 00:09:08.950
Well, yes.

00:09:08.950 --> 00:09:11.140
But if you take
a somebody that's

00:09:11.140 --> 00:09:14.740
never grown up in
any culture at all.

00:09:14.740 --> 00:09:16.120
They're totally helpless.

00:09:16.120 --> 00:09:17.440
They're about like this.

00:09:17.440 --> 00:09:19.120
So he says, well,
why don't you look

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:22.690
at the kind of things
that do help produce

00:09:22.690 --> 00:09:24.124
more capability for people?

00:09:24.124 --> 00:09:25.540
Well, you've got
a whole long list

00:09:25.540 --> 00:09:28.970
of that, including all the
technologies you can think of.

00:09:28.970 --> 00:09:32.650
And so I was making a list of
that back in the early '60s

00:09:32.650 --> 00:09:34.600
just to get organized
about how you really

00:09:34.600 --> 00:09:37.300
can amplify human capabilities.

00:09:37.300 --> 00:09:38.150
And oh, my gosh.

00:09:38.150 --> 00:09:40.960
But always, I began
to realize, oh, look.

00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:43.600
There are a few other
things, too, that are there.

00:09:47.530 --> 00:09:49.760
So there was no name for that.

00:09:49.760 --> 00:09:52.300
So I'd been calling
it the human system.

00:09:52.300 --> 00:09:55.960
And then it becomes
pretty clear that there's

00:09:55.960 --> 00:09:59.500
a huge amount of what we
call augmentation over here,

00:09:59.500 --> 00:10:01.990
as well as that and
the tool system.

00:10:01.990 --> 00:10:09.190
So the thing [INAUDIBLE] talks
about an explosive technologies

00:10:09.190 --> 00:10:11.530
on this right
side, and one looks

00:10:11.530 --> 00:10:18.850
at how things such as the
plow or the weaving looms

00:10:18.850 --> 00:10:22.510
or the printing press came
in on the right hand side.

00:10:22.510 --> 00:10:24.760
And the ensuing
evolution on the left

00:10:24.760 --> 00:10:26.800
hand side in order
to harness that

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:29.800
created big changes in there.

00:10:29.800 --> 00:10:34.090
So that the scaling thing is
becoming really important.

00:10:34.090 --> 00:10:38.800
And I'd actually done a study of
scaling stuff in the late '50s,

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:41.154
knowing that digital
technologies were going

00:10:41.154 --> 00:10:43.570
to have to get smaller and
smaller and faster and cheaper,

00:10:43.570 --> 00:10:45.640
and that was going
to come by learning

00:10:45.640 --> 00:10:47.510
how to build them much smaller.

00:10:47.510 --> 00:10:52.510
So I got a chance to do a few
months of study about scaling.

00:10:52.510 --> 00:10:54.910
Because once I had worked
at Ames Aeronautical lab,

00:10:54.910 --> 00:10:57.730
my first job out of
school like that,

00:10:57.730 --> 00:11:01.180
and had known aeronautics
people and talking about

00:11:01.180 --> 00:11:03.790
Reynolds numbers and things
like that, and that you

00:11:03.790 --> 00:11:07.430
had to get the same Reynolds
number in all the pressure,

00:11:07.430 --> 00:11:10.140
so that the scale
model would give you

00:11:10.140 --> 00:11:14.230
relatively valuable data, so
I learned a lot more about all

00:11:14.230 --> 00:11:15.170
of that.

00:11:15.170 --> 00:11:17.800
And one of the real things
that came out of that

00:11:17.800 --> 00:11:23.110
was, if you're used to building
and working with devices

00:11:23.110 --> 00:11:27.000
at a given scale, and suddenly
a change of scale in some

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:30.370
of the important parameters,
as it changes slowly,

00:11:30.370 --> 00:11:33.010
some of the quantitative
characteristics will change.

00:11:33.010 --> 00:11:35.700
But past a certain point
it gets qualitative,

00:11:35.700 --> 00:11:37.720
and things won't work anymore.

00:11:37.720 --> 00:11:41.890
And so I would try
to give lectures.

00:11:41.890 --> 00:11:44.320
And I would just tell people,
well, suppose everything

00:11:44.320 --> 00:11:48.850
in this room, including all the
people, just suddenly scaled up

00:11:48.850 --> 00:11:51.880
in size by a factor of 10
and have three dimensions,

00:11:51.880 --> 00:11:53.590
would you notice it?

00:11:53.590 --> 00:11:57.020
And so everybody
says, well, let's see.

00:11:57.020 --> 00:12:01.000
It'd be the same angle subtended
by anybody's-- they'd be 10

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:03.540
times as far away,
but 10 times as big.

00:12:03.540 --> 00:12:05.350
So I probably wouldn't notice.

00:12:05.350 --> 00:12:08.390
But then you start pointing
out, how much more do you weigh?

00:12:08.390 --> 00:12:10.150
Well, 10 times 10 times 10.

00:12:10.150 --> 00:12:11.960
That's a thousand times as much.

00:12:11.960 --> 00:12:12.460
Oh.

00:12:12.460 --> 00:12:14.049
How much stronger are you?

00:12:14.049 --> 00:12:14.590
I don't know.

00:12:14.590 --> 00:12:16.006
Well, somebody in
there would know

00:12:16.006 --> 00:12:18.940
that the strength of
your bones and muscles

00:12:18.940 --> 00:12:21.520
is really proportional to
the cross-section area.

00:12:21.520 --> 00:12:23.500
So you're only a
hundred times as strong,

00:12:23.500 --> 00:12:25.780
and your chair is only a
hundred times as strong.

00:12:25.780 --> 00:12:28.600
So that means, unless your
chair is designed with a safety

00:12:28.600 --> 00:12:30.849
factor of 10, you're doomed.

00:12:30.849 --> 00:12:31.890
So you fall on the floor.

00:12:31.890 --> 00:12:34.390
So your bones will break
because they can't stand it.

00:12:34.390 --> 00:12:37.540
And your lungs are only
a hundred times as large

00:12:37.540 --> 00:12:41.660
for a thousand times
metabolism needs.

00:12:41.660 --> 00:12:44.420
So you'd be dead pretty soon.

00:12:44.420 --> 00:12:48.550
But anyway, this
surprising set of things,

00:12:48.550 --> 00:12:51.370
when you're not used to
thinking about that scaling,

00:12:51.370 --> 00:12:53.770
so that really came home
to me in that study.

00:12:53.770 --> 00:12:57.460
So I looked at this
diagram, and I said, wow.

00:12:57.460 --> 00:13:00.910
With the scale of change that's
erupting and forthcoming here

00:13:00.910 --> 00:13:03.730
with the nanotechnology
and the digital stuff,

00:13:03.730 --> 00:13:07.780
that the changes over here that
are going to be taking place,

00:13:07.780 --> 00:13:08.620
some force--

00:13:08.620 --> 00:13:10.400
because as you adopt
all this and this

00:13:10.400 --> 00:13:11.950
it gets all of the turmoil here.

00:13:11.950 --> 00:13:14.710
But some of the change is
to take advantage of that.

00:13:14.710 --> 00:13:17.380
There are going to be a
lot of surprises in that.

00:13:17.380 --> 00:13:19.630
And so that's what I
started really to pursue,

00:13:19.630 --> 00:13:25.480
and especially in this language
in the getting your concepts

00:13:25.480 --> 00:13:29.680
symbolized, externalized,
manipulated in brand new ways,

00:13:29.680 --> 00:13:33.040
and realizing that the way
we're used to things as they're

00:13:33.040 --> 00:13:35.980
done in print,
that's just got very

00:13:35.980 --> 00:13:39.490
little to do about how
effectively you're harnessing

00:13:39.490 --> 00:13:41.352
your basic mechanisms in there.

00:13:41.352 --> 00:13:43.060
So you ought to be
able to read much more

00:13:43.060 --> 00:13:46.930
flexibly, getting the views
you want, moving around.

00:13:46.930 --> 00:13:48.460
Much leads on there.

00:13:48.460 --> 00:13:51.610
But pretty soon you're doing
things that look hard to learn

00:13:51.610 --> 00:13:54.160
and it's unnatural to use.

00:13:54.160 --> 00:13:57.580
So the mantra at the
time was easy to learn

00:13:57.580 --> 00:13:59.080
and natural to use.

00:13:59.080 --> 00:14:01.930
And that's been inhibiting
our exploration of this just

00:14:01.930 --> 00:14:03.115
immensely.

00:14:03.115 --> 00:14:04.740
And even in the the
Defense Department,

00:14:04.740 --> 00:14:08.059
I would go into DARPA and try to
talk about some of the things,

00:14:08.059 --> 00:14:10.350
and they'll say, oh, you gave
us this great system that

00:14:10.350 --> 00:14:12.170
will help people in a crisis.

00:14:12.170 --> 00:14:13.570
But they have to
be able to learn

00:14:13.570 --> 00:14:15.610
how to use it in 20 minutes.

00:14:15.610 --> 00:14:16.110
See?

00:14:16.110 --> 00:14:16.800
And he says, oh.

00:14:16.800 --> 00:14:18.270
Well, let's see.

00:14:18.270 --> 00:14:20.130
You've got some other
important missions

00:14:20.130 --> 00:14:22.770
during times like
that, like perhaps

00:14:22.770 --> 00:14:24.650
interceptor planes, right?

00:14:24.650 --> 00:14:25.440
Yeah.

00:14:25.440 --> 00:14:27.555
So with an important
mission like that,

00:14:27.555 --> 00:14:29.580
do you expect any
Jeep driver to learn

00:14:29.580 --> 00:14:32.550
how to fly that
airplane in 15 minutes?

00:14:32.550 --> 00:14:33.360
Well, no.

00:14:33.360 --> 00:14:34.200
But--

00:14:34.200 --> 00:14:38.400
So anyway, the perspective
about how important missions

00:14:38.400 --> 00:14:43.360
would be in handling complex
knowledge work, it just says,

00:14:43.360 --> 00:14:44.500
hey, wait a minute.

00:14:44.500 --> 00:14:47.370
It will be very important to go
after high performance stuff.

00:14:47.370 --> 00:14:49.230
And there's just a
lot in this domain

00:14:49.230 --> 00:14:51.150
that we could talk
about all day.

00:14:51.150 --> 00:14:54.500
But we're not going to.

00:14:54.500 --> 00:14:57.330
And so, any capability
your organization

00:14:57.330 --> 00:15:00.330
has depends on the co-evolution
of that thing, which

00:15:00.330 --> 00:15:03.420
includes stuff in
your world, but also

00:15:03.420 --> 00:15:08.210
even in athletics world or just
general business operations.

00:15:08.210 --> 00:15:13.750
So there is this something
that I did was say, oh--

00:15:13.750 --> 00:15:16.600
very crudely, because
each tool system

00:15:16.600 --> 00:15:19.540
has many dimensions to itself,
and so does the human system,

00:15:19.540 --> 00:15:22.540
but if you just do it a
little bit roughly like this,

00:15:22.540 --> 00:15:26.240
you'd say, I've got
a human tool space.

00:15:26.240 --> 00:15:29.410
And one's organization would
fit in there someplace.

00:15:29.410 --> 00:15:31.540
So what if we go around
the world looking

00:15:31.540 --> 00:15:34.150
at different societies
and see where they fit?

00:15:34.150 --> 00:15:35.890
Some use more or
less technology,

00:15:35.890 --> 00:15:39.650
and more advanced human
system development like that.

00:15:39.650 --> 00:15:40.370
And we say, okay.

00:15:40.370 --> 00:15:42.220
There are distributions, right?

00:15:42.220 --> 00:15:44.680
And someplace in there,
there's the organization

00:15:44.680 --> 00:15:46.960
you belong to that you're
probably pretty proud of.

00:15:46.960 --> 00:15:49.660
And you think, it's right
out near the frontier.

00:15:49.660 --> 00:15:50.550
See?

00:15:50.550 --> 00:15:53.830
We can anticipate today
these different changes

00:15:53.830 --> 00:15:57.130
in the technology and in
the human systems stuff.

00:15:57.130 --> 00:16:00.070
And we're a little bit
lagging in the human system.

00:16:00.070 --> 00:16:04.270
But boy, LAI is taking care of
us, moving us into here, right?

00:16:04.270 --> 00:16:09.850
Well then, let's just say there
is a distribution like that.

00:16:09.850 --> 00:16:13.020
And then let's talk about the
scaling business a little bit.

00:16:13.020 --> 00:16:16.240
It says, well,
now wait a minute.

00:16:16.240 --> 00:16:18.370
What if we really think
that technology is

00:16:18.370 --> 00:16:21.650
moving at a rate that's faster?

00:16:21.650 --> 00:16:25.900
Well, our whole way of
changing our organizations

00:16:25.900 --> 00:16:27.640
evolved over
centuries and thinking

00:16:27.640 --> 00:16:31.340
about where these boundaries
weren't that far away.

00:16:31.340 --> 00:16:36.310
But what if-- this is
why the paradigm shifted

00:16:36.310 --> 00:16:37.360
in people's minds.

00:16:37.360 --> 00:16:39.970
And they realized,
oh, wait a minute.

00:16:39.970 --> 00:16:42.040
Here we are with
our distribution.

00:16:42.040 --> 00:16:44.200
And here's all that you
can anticipate today

00:16:44.200 --> 00:16:47.890
and the technology, with
commensurate changes in here.

00:16:47.890 --> 00:16:51.250
We suddenly have a new frontier.

00:16:51.250 --> 00:16:53.920
So we should have
different paradigms

00:16:53.920 --> 00:16:57.670
about how we move our
organizations into that.

00:16:57.670 --> 00:16:59.920
And then somebody else
will come and look at it,

00:16:59.920 --> 00:17:02.750
like this guy from
California, and he'll

00:17:02.750 --> 00:17:09.700
say, well, why don't you just
be a little bolder about that?

00:17:09.700 --> 00:17:13.510
And you says, hey,
we haven't really

00:17:13.510 --> 00:17:16.310
seen what
nanotechnology will do.

00:17:16.310 --> 00:17:19.530
And it's just, that
scaling things down, it's

00:17:19.530 --> 00:17:23.050
just going to produce
capacity and capabilities

00:17:23.050 --> 00:17:27.430
and bandwidth and everything
else that we never dreamed of.

00:17:27.430 --> 00:17:29.500
And oh, I see.

00:17:29.500 --> 00:17:30.477
And that's something.

00:17:30.477 --> 00:17:32.060
How is that going
to affect the world?

00:17:32.060 --> 00:17:33.370
Well, okay.

00:17:33.370 --> 00:17:34.970
What about computers
and things will

00:17:34.970 --> 00:17:37.992
get so small that you could
surgically implant them

00:17:37.992 --> 00:17:39.700
and they can get the
energy to drive them

00:17:39.700 --> 00:17:42.640
from your metabolism, and they
could connect to your nerves,

00:17:42.640 --> 00:17:44.830
and they can also have
sensor things that

00:17:44.830 --> 00:17:47.950
go beyond what your sensory
capabilities can do?

00:17:47.950 --> 00:17:49.810
And all that connects in.

00:17:49.810 --> 00:17:54.609
And boy, you could become,
what do they call them, cyborgs

00:17:54.609 --> 00:17:55.150
or something?

00:17:55.150 --> 00:17:56.240
That's really tremendous.

00:17:56.240 --> 00:17:59.970
But the scale of things is
going to be able to do that.

00:17:59.970 --> 00:18:04.180
Just a couple examples out of
the air here like that, see?

00:18:04.180 --> 00:18:07.722
And then intelligent agents
are really just inevitable,

00:18:07.722 --> 00:18:08.430
going to come in.

00:18:08.430 --> 00:18:09.880
And they're beginning to.

00:18:09.880 --> 00:18:12.225
And hey, how are you going
to learn how often you've

00:18:12.225 --> 00:18:14.530
got a whole crew of slaves?

00:18:14.530 --> 00:18:18.160
It's not realistic to
think of them just sort

00:18:18.160 --> 00:18:20.530
of bumping along and
helping do a spell

00:18:20.530 --> 00:18:22.190
check or some things like that.

00:18:22.190 --> 00:18:23.680
They're going to
be things that you

00:18:23.680 --> 00:18:27.250
can learn how to steer and
guide and will interact with.

00:18:27.250 --> 00:18:29.350
And it will take
skill and learning

00:18:29.350 --> 00:18:32.260
how to do that, but
boost your power a lot.

00:18:32.260 --> 00:18:37.220
So he says, oh, that
frontier is more like this.

00:18:37.220 --> 00:18:41.930
The relative scale-- and
none of our processes

00:18:41.930 --> 00:18:46.070
for social or business
adaptation, they all

00:18:46.070 --> 00:18:50.570
were evolved with the frontier
being very near like that.

00:18:50.570 --> 00:18:54.150
So it takes a real
call to re-examine

00:18:54.150 --> 00:18:56.460
what's the processes?

00:18:56.460 --> 00:19:00.380
What are the processes by which
our organizations can adapt

00:19:00.380 --> 00:19:03.440
and start harnessing this
thing, and our societies learn

00:19:03.440 --> 00:19:05.240
how to cope with it?

00:19:05.240 --> 00:19:10.290
So we need to look at different
processes for our evolving.

00:19:10.290 --> 00:19:13.190
And this has led me
to that bootstrapping.

00:19:13.190 --> 00:19:15.260
And then it leads
to things like,

00:19:15.260 --> 00:19:18.590
oh, let's re-examine the
infrastructure by which

00:19:18.590 --> 00:19:21.110
our organizations
improve and see

00:19:21.110 --> 00:19:24.770
if the improvement
infrastructure can be improved,

00:19:24.770 --> 00:19:26.510
and then if you
can bootstrap it.

00:19:26.510 --> 00:19:29.580
So that's the message.

00:19:29.580 --> 00:19:32.750
And it turned out that,
for the formulation

00:19:32.750 --> 00:19:37.190
we evolved, that one of the key
elements in that improvement

00:19:37.190 --> 00:19:40.130
infrastructure is what
we called an improvement

00:19:40.130 --> 00:19:44.000
community, something to
have special focus on.

00:19:44.000 --> 00:19:47.000
And LAI is an
improvement community.

00:19:47.000 --> 00:19:50.030
And then it's saying
that look, if we

00:19:50.030 --> 00:19:52.820
can bring the
modernization work,

00:19:52.820 --> 00:19:55.550
especially in collective
knowledge work,

00:19:55.550 --> 00:19:59.270
into appropriate
improvement communities,

00:19:59.270 --> 00:20:03.890
this is going to give us
that extra second degree

00:20:03.890 --> 00:20:05.420
acceleration or
something like that.

00:20:05.420 --> 00:20:08.180
It gives extra
strategic leverage

00:20:08.180 --> 00:20:10.040
in the improvement process.

00:20:10.040 --> 00:20:12.690
And so that's one of the reasons
why, when I talked to Earl,

00:20:12.690 --> 00:20:14.050
I thought oh, boy.

00:20:14.050 --> 00:20:18.620
There's a high tech
improvement community

00:20:18.620 --> 00:20:22.820
and it's based in MIT, which
has a few people that are used

00:20:22.820 --> 00:20:25.730
to working with information.

00:20:25.730 --> 00:20:27.530
And it's a great model.

00:20:27.530 --> 00:20:29.500
So let's talk a little
more about that.

00:20:32.670 --> 00:20:36.600
So one of the strategic things
that somehow there have to be

00:20:36.600 --> 00:20:41.400
is ways to establish outposts
out in that frontier.

00:20:41.400 --> 00:20:45.840
And it isn't a matter of
going out there and setting up

00:20:45.840 --> 00:20:48.480
a group of people that,
by themselves, are just

00:20:48.480 --> 00:20:50.700
doing startling things.

00:20:50.700 --> 00:20:54.720
The way that that's going to
show organizations how to go

00:20:54.720 --> 00:20:57.780
is if those are doing real work.

00:20:57.780 --> 00:21:00.990
And it'd be very handy if
the real work they're doing

00:21:00.990 --> 00:21:05.490
is a visible part of the
work the organizations are

00:21:05.490 --> 00:21:07.420
doing already.

00:21:07.420 --> 00:21:09.360
So that means you
don't set them up

00:21:09.360 --> 00:21:12.720
in a laboratory in a glass bowl
doing their special things.

00:21:12.720 --> 00:21:15.145
So there are a lot of
thinking about that.

00:21:15.145 --> 00:21:17.270
And how do you get the high
performance groups that

00:21:17.270 --> 00:21:18.464
are going to be doing that?

00:21:18.464 --> 00:21:19.380
How are they composed?

00:21:19.380 --> 00:21:20.463
What are they going to do?

00:21:20.463 --> 00:21:23.680
So all of that is an important
part of thinking this way.

00:21:23.680 --> 00:21:25.540
So we've talked
about this picture

00:21:25.540 --> 00:21:27.360
of the co-evolution frontier.

00:21:27.360 --> 00:21:30.270
And let's talk about, then,
one of the very important

00:21:30.270 --> 00:21:31.935
improvement categories--

00:21:31.935 --> 00:21:33.860
the strategic, in there--

00:21:33.860 --> 00:21:37.180
as a collective intelligence.

00:21:37.180 --> 00:21:40.030
So let's say you have
a bunch of people,

00:21:40.030 --> 00:21:43.570
and they're involved some
organization in this mandala

00:21:43.570 --> 00:21:47.890
kind of thing in here with
all of the cross connections

00:21:47.890 --> 00:21:52.570
implying that every member
of this organization

00:21:52.570 --> 00:21:55.240
needs to have a channel
for communicating

00:21:55.240 --> 00:21:57.990
with every other one.

00:21:57.990 --> 00:22:00.560
And so that's the way
they work together.

00:22:00.560 --> 00:22:02.960
Then it's obvious
that if they're

00:22:02.960 --> 00:22:05.340
going to get
collectively smarter,

00:22:05.340 --> 00:22:07.520
they have to manage
the knowledge in there

00:22:07.520 --> 00:22:11.150
and, in the traditional
sense, treating a document

00:22:11.150 --> 00:22:14.990
as a container with some
authored knowledge in it

00:22:14.990 --> 00:22:17.630
like that, you said, well,
it's going to be documents.

00:22:17.630 --> 00:22:20.390
And with the way the web
has taught people like that,

00:22:20.390 --> 00:22:24.440
it's easy now to tell them
that the web is a beautiful way

00:22:24.440 --> 00:22:26.780
to start managing
their knowledge.

00:22:26.780 --> 00:22:30.650
And how do you go about,
then, making them smarter?

00:22:30.650 --> 00:22:33.980
Well, that's something we
could talk for hours and hours

00:22:33.980 --> 00:22:35.120
about, too.

00:22:35.120 --> 00:22:37.670
But the experience
and the motivations

00:22:37.670 --> 00:22:42.000
and the things we experimented
with just really show up a lot.

00:22:42.000 --> 00:22:47.300
And so it's how do you really
move beyond the knowledge work

00:22:47.300 --> 00:22:52.100
capabilities that the
vendors can offer you,

00:22:52.100 --> 00:22:58.850
because the user organizations
aren't yet proactively up ahead

00:22:58.850 --> 00:23:02.420
enough to say, I want to start
learning about the really

00:23:02.420 --> 00:23:05.840
far out things, rather
than the way it is now

00:23:05.840 --> 00:23:07.580
that what we're
going to think about

00:23:07.580 --> 00:23:10.190
is what we can implement
today or tomorrow?

00:23:10.190 --> 00:23:13.490
And we have the political
problems of conflict

00:23:13.490 --> 00:23:14.930
inside our
organization of people

00:23:14.930 --> 00:23:16.820
that don't want to have
to change that much,

00:23:16.820 --> 00:23:22.130
or are suspicious, all kinds
of what you end up realizing

00:23:22.130 --> 00:23:30.530
are very natural cultural
impedances for that.

00:23:30.530 --> 00:23:34.340
And so in the end, there's
going to be a dynamic knowledge

00:23:34.340 --> 00:23:39.350
repository that's electronic,
hyper-media linked, like that.

00:23:39.350 --> 00:23:43.550
And the ways for
organizations to manage that,

00:23:43.550 --> 00:23:46.380
they want to be a lot more
alert about the outside world.

00:23:46.380 --> 00:23:49.760
So they're going to have to
be able to go out and actively

00:23:49.760 --> 00:23:53.390
ingest and assess and organize
the intelligence about what

00:23:53.390 --> 00:23:55.850
the outside world is doing.

00:23:55.850 --> 00:23:58.940
And this social
organism that you

00:23:58.940 --> 00:24:04.040
call an organization is going
to be judged on its IQ a lot

00:24:04.040 --> 00:24:07.490
by, oh, how sensitive
and perceptual

00:24:07.490 --> 00:24:10.880
is it about what's going on in
the outside world that offers

00:24:10.880 --> 00:24:13.020
opportunities or threats?

00:24:13.020 --> 00:24:18.220
And when either of those
occurs, how smart and clever

00:24:18.220 --> 00:24:21.260
are their plan for how to cope?

00:24:21.260 --> 00:24:24.770
And how well do they, then,
marshal their resources

00:24:24.770 --> 00:24:27.260
and coordinate them in coping?

00:24:27.260 --> 00:24:30.620
And how quick are they
to re-adapt and shift?

00:24:30.620 --> 00:24:34.010
And you begin realizing the
way the future is going to go,

00:24:34.010 --> 00:24:37.550
that the annual yearly
budget thing is probably

00:24:37.550 --> 00:24:39.560
going to have to
go by the boards

00:24:39.560 --> 00:24:41.030
and become a much more dynamic.

00:24:41.030 --> 00:24:43.610
And a lot of things
are changing.

00:24:43.610 --> 00:24:47.240
The recorded dialogue and the
knowledge product are just,

00:24:47.240 --> 00:24:50.750
they're a way to say, oh,
any given time you really

00:24:50.750 --> 00:24:54.075
want all the plans, the designs,
the current state of things,

00:24:54.075 --> 00:24:55.700
the current state of
your understanding

00:24:55.700 --> 00:24:58.280
about the outside world, the
current state of the scenarios

00:24:58.280 --> 00:25:01.940
that you're depending
upon, is all sort

00:25:01.940 --> 00:25:04.910
of what you call knowledge
products, available for people

00:25:04.910 --> 00:25:06.950
to use in there.

00:25:06.950 --> 00:25:09.200
And how they study it
and how it's organized

00:25:09.200 --> 00:25:12.380
is a whole field and
exploration itself.

00:25:12.380 --> 00:25:14.630
And then at any
given time, there's

00:25:14.630 --> 00:25:16.310
a lot of dialogue about it.

00:25:16.310 --> 00:25:19.810
And you're in the midst of a
dialogue exercise right now,

00:25:19.810 --> 00:25:21.790
this morning.

00:25:21.790 --> 00:25:25.600
And recording that--
well, what I say

00:25:25.600 --> 00:25:27.540
and what I present
here like that,

00:25:27.540 --> 00:25:29.800
the technology is already
available for that

00:25:29.800 --> 00:25:33.820
to get nailed down
and be on a web,

00:25:33.820 --> 00:25:35.845
and automatically
transcribing my speech

00:25:35.845 --> 00:25:39.260
as much well enough at least
so that you can scan for that.

00:25:39.260 --> 00:25:41.650
So you can go back
and then find out

00:25:41.650 --> 00:25:44.200
what I said and look at
it again, and say, oh

00:25:44.200 --> 00:25:45.260
yeah, that's interesting.

00:25:45.260 --> 00:25:49.030
And then the way you can make
comments on it are the links

00:25:49.030 --> 00:25:51.880
that you'll be able to provide
can point to a particular

00:25:51.880 --> 00:25:55.020
passage that I spoke-- and
its associated diagram--

00:25:55.020 --> 00:25:56.500
or run up and down that.

00:25:56.500 --> 00:26:00.160
Or cleverly say, hey, I want to
hear what he says and then show

00:26:00.160 --> 00:26:03.370
the three kind of
slide pictures that he

00:26:03.370 --> 00:26:04.810
showed that went on that.

00:26:04.810 --> 00:26:06.850
And then I'd like, when
you follow that link,

00:26:06.850 --> 00:26:08.950
to highlight something
there because that's

00:26:08.950 --> 00:26:10.630
what I like to talk about.

00:26:10.630 --> 00:26:12.760
Well, that's the
kind of linked things

00:26:12.760 --> 00:26:14.890
that were part of our plan.

00:26:14.890 --> 00:26:17.920
And the basic
underlying standards

00:26:17.920 --> 00:26:20.830
for the extended
markup language as such

00:26:20.830 --> 00:26:22.720
will provide things like that.

00:26:22.720 --> 00:26:27.640
So then people make comments
on this and questions,

00:26:27.640 --> 00:26:31.000
and the dialogue's comment
on the comments grows.

00:26:31.000 --> 00:26:34.810
So these are all things that
technology can offer today.

00:26:34.810 --> 00:26:37.660
But it's just a
introductory example

00:26:37.660 --> 00:26:41.590
of this recorded dialogue
is a dynamic part

00:26:41.590 --> 00:26:45.460
of integrating intelligence and
arguing about what's there now,

00:26:45.460 --> 00:26:49.390
by which your knowledge
product evolves and changes.

00:26:49.390 --> 00:26:54.490
So every organization that
survives into very far

00:26:54.490 --> 00:26:56.200
in the next century
it's going to have

00:26:56.200 --> 00:26:58.940
to start living like this.

00:26:58.940 --> 00:27:02.390
So there are a few other things
to think about in this and say,

00:27:02.390 --> 00:27:03.380
oh.

00:27:03.380 --> 00:27:08.480
You know, if I'm a division or
a part of a bigger organization,

00:27:08.480 --> 00:27:10.970
my division or
project or program

00:27:10.970 --> 00:27:14.190
is going to have its own
knowledge repository that's

00:27:14.190 --> 00:27:19.440
got to be dynamic and totally
relevant to what we're doing.

00:27:19.440 --> 00:27:22.130
And yet, the whole
organization does, too.

00:27:22.130 --> 00:27:24.200
And so every one of
these nodes around here

00:27:24.200 --> 00:27:30.020
has its own dynamic repository,
as well as the whole thing.

00:27:30.020 --> 00:27:33.740
And so all of those are
evolving concurrently,

00:27:33.740 --> 00:27:36.950
and yet need to stay in
sync, that none of them

00:27:36.950 --> 00:27:43.610
is looking at a future of
something that isn't in any way

00:27:43.610 --> 00:27:47.010
contrary to any of the others.

00:27:47.010 --> 00:27:51.320
So it's that concurrency thing
that really hits the demand.

00:27:51.320 --> 00:27:52.967
Yet you don't hear
that very often.

00:27:52.967 --> 00:27:54.800
You know they have to
have interoperability,

00:27:54.800 --> 00:27:56.400
but not scaling.

00:27:56.400 --> 00:27:58.520
So the challenge is large.

00:27:58.520 --> 00:28:00.290
And the challenge
there is something

00:28:00.290 --> 00:28:03.980
that you don't just get by
getting a new product out

00:28:03.980 --> 00:28:05.780
by a given vendor or something.

00:28:05.780 --> 00:28:08.680
It's got to be
something that evolves

00:28:08.680 --> 00:28:11.640
in a very different way.

00:28:11.640 --> 00:28:15.020
And so we ended up with
this acronym CoDIAK,

00:28:15.020 --> 00:28:18.920
saying that describes a
core set of capabilities

00:28:18.920 --> 00:28:22.190
in the future
smart organization,

00:28:22.190 --> 00:28:25.025
that they concurrently develop,
integrate, and apply knowledge.

00:28:25.025 --> 00:28:26.790
It's the best we
could sort of do.

00:28:26.790 --> 00:28:29.300
So we use that term
a lot, and say,

00:28:29.300 --> 00:28:31.670
how do you go about
improving that?

00:28:31.670 --> 00:28:35.780
And you can just guess
that every one, almost

00:28:35.780 --> 00:28:39.850
every objective you'd like to
set up for what LAI can do,

00:28:39.850 --> 00:28:42.070
can do a lot better if the
organizations were better

00:28:42.070 --> 00:28:43.040
at this.

00:28:43.040 --> 00:28:44.570
And a very important thing--

00:28:44.570 --> 00:28:48.380
oh, LAI could operate
a lot more effectively.

00:28:48.380 --> 00:28:51.650
So where should you put your
initial exploratory energy

00:28:51.650 --> 00:28:55.200
about doing outposts that are
trying to learn how to do this?

00:28:55.200 --> 00:28:57.410
Well, strategically
speaking, LAI

00:28:57.410 --> 00:29:00.680
is the place to start
investing in learning advanced

00:29:00.680 --> 00:29:02.250
how to do this.

00:29:02.250 --> 00:29:06.200
So I got some great lessons
interacting with the McDonnell

00:29:06.200 --> 00:29:06.920
Douglas people.

00:29:06.920 --> 00:29:09.050
So they were showing
me that, well,

00:29:09.050 --> 00:29:11.240
so you have a couple
thousand people working

00:29:11.240 --> 00:29:14.230
inside of the McDonnell
plant for on a fighter plane.

00:29:14.230 --> 00:29:17.060
And look at what you have
in these tiers of suppliers

00:29:17.060 --> 00:29:20.010
and the kind of interactions
and design coordination

00:29:20.010 --> 00:29:21.480
that have to go on there.

00:29:21.480 --> 00:29:24.560
So there's a real
example for concurrency

00:29:24.560 --> 00:29:27.260
and interoperability
requirement.

00:29:27.260 --> 00:29:30.560
So these are things that
you very rarely, very rarely

00:29:30.560 --> 00:29:34.350
hear about when vendors
are selling you things.

00:29:34.350 --> 00:29:38.610
But this is the
real marketplace.

00:29:42.220 --> 00:29:45.330
So anyway, this open
hyper-document system

00:29:45.330 --> 00:29:49.870
is something that evolved in
our minds like 14 years ago

00:29:49.870 --> 00:29:51.970
at McDonnell Douglas
about saying,

00:29:51.970 --> 00:29:54.520
you really, really
have to come out

00:29:54.520 --> 00:29:57.550
with a standard, open,
hyper-document system.

00:29:57.550 --> 00:30:00.130
And the HTML offered
a beautiful way

00:30:00.130 --> 00:30:02.460
to get a standard
way for publishing.

00:30:02.460 --> 00:30:05.200
And the XML is
starting to advance it.

00:30:05.200 --> 00:30:07.570
And most of that is
being done by vendors,

00:30:07.570 --> 00:30:09.580
the evolution of standards.

00:30:09.580 --> 00:30:12.460
And what is critically
needed, really,

00:30:12.460 --> 00:30:16.180
is the proactive
involvement of the end user

00:30:16.180 --> 00:30:19.690
organizations in the
evolution of the standards

00:30:19.690 --> 00:30:20.890
for this thing.

00:30:20.890 --> 00:30:23.530
So these are sort
of the properties

00:30:23.530 --> 00:30:25.780
that all these things.

00:30:25.780 --> 00:30:29.440
And then it becomes
pretty clear that there's

00:30:29.440 --> 00:30:32.980
a huge amount of what we
call augmentation over here,

00:30:32.980 --> 00:30:35.440
as well as that in
the tool system.

00:30:35.440 --> 00:30:42.640
So the thing then, if one talks
about an explosive technologies

00:30:42.640 --> 00:30:44.980
on this right
side, and one looks

00:30:44.980 --> 00:30:52.330
at how things such as the
plow or the weaving looms

00:30:52.330 --> 00:30:55.990
or the printing press came
in on the right hand side,

00:30:55.990 --> 00:30:58.420
and the ensuing evolution
in the left hand

00:30:58.420 --> 00:31:03.280
side in order to harness that
created big changes in there.

00:31:03.280 --> 00:31:07.430
So that if this scaling thing
is becoming really important--

00:31:07.430 --> 00:31:12.250
and I'd actually done a study of
scaling stuff in the late '50s,

00:31:12.250 --> 00:31:14.634
knowing that digital
technologies were going

00:31:14.634 --> 00:31:17.050
to have to get smaller and
smaller and faster and cheaper,

00:31:17.050 --> 00:31:19.120
and that was going
to come by learning

00:31:19.120 --> 00:31:20.990
how to build them much smaller.

00:31:20.990 --> 00:31:25.990
So I got a chance to do a few
months of study about scaling.

00:31:25.990 --> 00:31:28.390
Because once I had worked
at Ames Aeronautical Lab,

00:31:28.390 --> 00:31:31.180
my first job out of
school like that,

00:31:31.180 --> 00:31:34.417
and had known the aeronautics
people and then talked

00:31:34.417 --> 00:31:36.250
about Reynolds numbers
and things like that,

00:31:36.250 --> 00:31:38.710
and that you know that you
had to get the same Reynolds

00:31:38.710 --> 00:31:42.550
number and all the pressure so
that the scale model would give

00:31:42.550 --> 00:31:45.460
you relatively valuable data.

00:31:45.460 --> 00:31:48.650
And so I learned a lot
more about all of that.

00:31:48.650 --> 00:31:51.280
And one of the real
things that came out of it

00:31:51.280 --> 00:31:56.590
was, if you're used to building
and working with devices

00:31:56.590 --> 00:32:00.460
at a given scale, and suddenly
the change of scale in some

00:32:00.460 --> 00:32:03.820
of the important parameters,
as it changes slowly,

00:32:03.820 --> 00:32:06.490
some of the quantitative
characteristics will change.

00:32:06.490 --> 00:32:09.190
But past a certain point,
it gets qualitative,

00:32:09.190 --> 00:32:11.200
and things won't work anymore.

00:32:11.200 --> 00:32:15.330
And so I would try
to give lectures.

00:32:15.330 --> 00:32:17.800
And I would just tell people,
well, suppose everything

00:32:17.800 --> 00:32:19.850
in this room--

00:32:19.850 --> 00:32:21.010
including all the people--

00:32:21.010 --> 00:32:23.920
just suddenly scaled up
in size by a factor of 10,

00:32:23.920 --> 00:32:25.360
and have three dimensions.

00:32:25.360 --> 00:32:27.070
Would you notice anything?

00:32:27.070 --> 00:32:30.586
And so everybody
says well, let's see.

00:32:30.586 --> 00:32:34.480
It'd be the same angle subtended
by anybody's-- they'd be 10

00:32:34.480 --> 00:32:37.430
times as far away
but 10 times as big.

00:32:37.430 --> 00:32:38.800
I probably wouldn't notice.

00:32:38.800 --> 00:32:41.890
But then you start pointing
out, how much more do you weigh?

00:32:41.890 --> 00:32:43.630
Well, 10 times 10 times 10.

00:32:43.630 --> 00:32:45.440
That's a thousand times as much.

00:32:45.440 --> 00:32:45.940
Oh.

00:32:45.940 --> 00:32:47.529
How much stronger are you?

00:32:47.529 --> 00:32:48.070
I don't know.

00:32:48.070 --> 00:32:49.486
Well, somebody in
there would know

00:32:49.486 --> 00:32:52.420
that the strength of
your bones and muscles

00:32:52.420 --> 00:32:55.000
is really proportional to
the cross-section area.

00:32:55.000 --> 00:32:56.950
So you're only a
hundred times as strong,

00:32:56.950 --> 00:32:59.260
and your chair's only a
hundred times as strong.

00:32:59.260 --> 00:33:02.080
So that means, unless your
chair was designed with a safety

00:33:02.080 --> 00:33:04.319
factor of 10, you're doomed.

00:33:04.319 --> 00:33:05.360
So you fall on the floor.

00:33:05.360 --> 00:33:07.870
So your bones will break,
because they can't stand it.

00:33:07.870 --> 00:33:11.250
And your lungs are only
a hundred times as large

00:33:11.250 --> 00:33:15.140
for a thousand times
metabolism needs.

00:33:15.140 --> 00:33:17.900
So you'll be dead pretty soon.

00:33:17.900 --> 00:33:22.030
But anyway, this
surprising set of things

00:33:22.030 --> 00:33:24.840
when you're not used to
thinking about that scaling,

00:33:24.840 --> 00:33:27.350
so that really came
home to me, that study.

00:33:27.350 --> 00:33:30.940
So I looked at this
diagram, and I said, wow.

00:33:30.940 --> 00:33:34.390
With the scale of change that's
erupting and forthcoming here

00:33:34.390 --> 00:33:37.180
with the nanotechnology
and the digital stuff,

00:33:37.180 --> 00:33:41.260
that the changes over here that
are going to be taking place,

00:33:41.260 --> 00:33:42.100
some force--

00:33:42.100 --> 00:33:43.880
because as you
adopt all and this

00:33:43.880 --> 00:33:45.430
it gets all the turmoil here.

00:33:45.430 --> 00:33:48.190
But some of the changes
to take advantage of that,

00:33:48.190 --> 00:33:50.860
there are going to be a
lot of surprises in that.

00:33:50.860 --> 00:33:53.110
And so that's what I
started really to pursue,

00:33:53.110 --> 00:33:55.600
and especially in this
language and in getting

00:33:55.600 --> 00:34:01.720
your concepts symbolized,
externalized, manipulated

00:34:01.720 --> 00:34:04.540
in brand new ways, and
realizing that the way we're

00:34:04.540 --> 00:34:08.920
used to things as they're
done in print, that's just

00:34:08.920 --> 00:34:12.969
got very little to do about how
effectively you're harnessing

00:34:12.969 --> 00:34:14.957
your basic mechanisms in there.

00:34:14.957 --> 00:34:16.540
So you ought be able
to read much more

00:34:16.540 --> 00:34:20.409
flexibly, getting the views
you want, moving around.

00:34:20.409 --> 00:34:21.940
Much leads on there.

00:34:21.940 --> 00:34:25.060
But pretty soon you're doing
things that look hard to learn

00:34:25.060 --> 00:34:27.620
and it's unnatural to use.

00:34:27.620 --> 00:34:31.060
So the mantra at the
time was easy to learn

00:34:31.060 --> 00:34:32.530
and natural to use.

00:34:32.530 --> 00:34:35.409
And that's been inhibiting
our exploration of this just

00:34:35.409 --> 00:34:36.969
immensely.

00:34:36.969 --> 00:34:39.550
And even in the Defense
Department, we'd go into DARPA

00:34:39.550 --> 00:34:42.355
and try to talk about some of
the things, and they'll say,

00:34:42.355 --> 00:34:43.730
oh, you give us
this great system

00:34:43.730 --> 00:34:45.530
that'll help people in crisis.

00:34:45.530 --> 00:34:47.030
But they have to
be able to learn

00:34:47.030 --> 00:34:49.290
how to use it in 20 minutes.

00:34:49.290 --> 00:34:49.790
See?

00:34:49.790 --> 00:34:51.739
And he says, oh well, let's see.

00:34:51.739 --> 00:34:53.600
You've got some other
important missions

00:34:53.600 --> 00:34:56.239
during times like
that, like perhaps

00:34:56.239 --> 00:34:58.100
interceptor planes, right?

00:34:58.100 --> 00:34:58.910
Yeah.

00:34:58.910 --> 00:35:01.040
So with an important
mission like that,

00:35:01.040 --> 00:35:03.050
do you expect any
Jeep driver to learn

00:35:03.050 --> 00:35:06.030
how to fly that
airplane in 15 minutes?

00:35:06.030 --> 00:35:06.830
Well, no.

00:35:06.830 --> 00:35:07.680
But--

00:35:07.680 --> 00:35:11.870
So anyway, the perspective
about how important missions

00:35:11.870 --> 00:35:16.830
would be in handling complex
knowledge work, it just says,

00:35:16.830 --> 00:35:17.970
hey, wait a minute.

00:35:17.970 --> 00:35:20.840
It will be very important to go
after high performance stuff.

00:35:20.840 --> 00:35:22.700
And there's just a
lot in this domain

00:35:22.700 --> 00:35:24.620
that we could talk
about all day.

00:35:24.620 --> 00:35:27.980
But we're not going to.

00:35:27.980 --> 00:35:30.830
And so any capability
your organization

00:35:30.830 --> 00:35:33.800
has depends on the co-evolution
of that thing, which

00:35:33.800 --> 00:35:36.890
includes stuff in
your world, but also

00:35:36.890 --> 00:35:41.780
even in athletics world or just
general business operations.

00:35:41.780 --> 00:35:47.240
So there is something
that I did was say, oh--

00:35:47.240 --> 00:35:50.090
very crudely, because
each, the tool system

00:35:50.090 --> 00:35:53.020
has many dimensions to itself,
and so does the human system.

00:35:53.020 --> 00:35:56.390
But if you just do it a little
bit roughly like this, you say,

00:35:56.390 --> 00:35:59.600
I've got a human tool space.

00:35:59.600 --> 00:36:02.870
And one's organization would
fit in there someplace.

00:36:02.870 --> 00:36:05.030
So what if we go around
the world looking

00:36:05.030 --> 00:36:07.640
at different societies
and see where they fit?

00:36:07.640 --> 00:36:11.600
Some use more or less technology
and more advanced human system

00:36:11.600 --> 00:36:13.130
development like that.

00:36:13.130 --> 00:36:15.680
And we say, okay, there
are distributions, right?

00:36:15.680 --> 00:36:18.140
And someplace in there,
there's the organization

00:36:18.140 --> 00:36:20.420
you belong to that you're
probably pretty proud of.

00:36:20.420 --> 00:36:23.120
And you think it's right
out near the frontier.

00:36:23.120 --> 00:36:23.830
See?

00:36:23.830 --> 00:36:27.320
We can anticipate today
these different changes

00:36:27.320 --> 00:36:30.620
in the technology and in
the human system stuff.

00:36:30.620 --> 00:36:33.530
And we're a little bit
lagging in the human system,

00:36:33.530 --> 00:36:37.730
but boy, LAI's taking care
of us, moving us into here.

00:36:37.730 --> 00:36:43.310
Well then, let's just say there
is a distribution like that.

00:36:43.310 --> 00:36:46.360
And then let's talk about the
scaling business a little bit.

00:36:46.360 --> 00:36:49.760
It says, well,
now wait a minute.

00:36:49.760 --> 00:36:51.860
What if we really think
that technology is

00:36:51.860 --> 00:36:55.140
moving at a rate that's faster?

00:36:55.140 --> 00:36:59.360
Well, our whole way of
changing our organizations

00:36:59.360 --> 00:37:01.100
evolved over
centuries and thinking

00:37:01.100 --> 00:37:04.786
about where these boundaries
weren't that far away.

00:37:04.786 --> 00:37:09.800
But what if this is why
the paradigm shifted

00:37:09.800 --> 00:37:13.110
in people's minds, and they
realized, oh wait a minute.

00:37:13.110 --> 00:37:15.500
Here we are with
our distribution.

00:37:15.500 --> 00:37:17.660
And here's all that you
can anticipate today

00:37:17.660 --> 00:37:21.380
in the technology, with
commensurate changes in here?

00:37:21.380 --> 00:37:24.740
We suddenly have a new frontier.

00:37:24.740 --> 00:37:27.410
So we should have
different paradigms

00:37:27.410 --> 00:37:31.130
about how we move our
organizations into that.

00:37:31.130 --> 00:37:33.410
And then somebody else
will come and look at it,

00:37:33.410 --> 00:37:36.230
like this guy from
California, and he'll

00:37:36.230 --> 00:37:42.110
say, well why don't you just
be a little bolder about that,

00:37:42.110 --> 00:37:43.140
then?

00:37:43.140 --> 00:37:46.970
And you says, hey,
we haven't really

00:37:46.970 --> 00:37:49.770
seen what
nanotechnology will do.

00:37:49.770 --> 00:37:53.000
And it's just that
scaling things down, it's

00:37:53.000 --> 00:37:56.510
just going to produce
capacity and capabilities

00:37:56.510 --> 00:38:00.920
and bandwidth and everything
else that we never dreamed of.

00:38:00.920 --> 00:38:02.990
And oh, I see.

00:38:02.990 --> 00:38:03.957
And that's something.

00:38:03.957 --> 00:38:05.540
How is that going
to affect the world?

00:38:05.540 --> 00:38:06.860
Well, okay.

00:38:06.860 --> 00:38:08.450
What about computers
and things will

00:38:08.450 --> 00:38:11.399
get so small that you could
surgically implant them

00:38:11.399 --> 00:38:13.190
and they could get the
energy to drive them

00:38:13.190 --> 00:38:16.130
from your metabolism, and they
could connect to your nerves,

00:38:16.130 --> 00:38:18.320
and they could also
have sensor things that

00:38:18.320 --> 00:38:21.410
go beyond what your sensory
capabilities can do,

00:38:21.410 --> 00:38:23.270
and all that connects in?

00:38:23.270 --> 00:38:28.099
And boy, you could become,
what do they call them, cyborgs

00:38:28.099 --> 00:38:28.640
or something?

00:38:28.640 --> 00:38:29.681
That's really tremendous.

00:38:29.681 --> 00:38:33.410
But the scale of things is
going to be able to do that.

00:38:33.410 --> 00:38:36.770
Just a couple examples out of
the air, and you're like that,

00:38:36.770 --> 00:38:37.670
see?

00:38:37.670 --> 00:38:41.090
And then intelligent agents
are really just inevitable,

00:38:41.090 --> 00:38:41.800
going to come in.

00:38:41.800 --> 00:38:43.340
And they're beginning to.

00:38:43.340 --> 00:38:45.080
And hey, how are
you going to learn

00:38:45.080 --> 00:38:47.990
how, if you've got a
whole crew of slaves,

00:38:47.990 --> 00:38:51.620
it's not realistic to
think of them just sort

00:38:51.620 --> 00:38:54.215
of bumping along and
helping do a spell check

00:38:54.215 --> 00:38:55.712
or some things like that.

00:38:55.712 --> 00:38:57.170
They're going to
be things that you

00:38:57.170 --> 00:39:00.710
can learn how to steer and
guide and will interact with,

00:39:00.710 --> 00:39:04.430
and will take skill and learning
how to do that, but boost

00:39:04.430 --> 00:39:05.750
your power a lot.

00:39:05.750 --> 00:39:07.190
So he says, oh.

00:39:07.190 --> 00:39:10.600
You know, that frontier
is more like this.

00:39:10.600 --> 00:39:15.410
The relative scale-- and
none of our processes

00:39:15.410 --> 00:39:19.550
for social or business
adaptation, they all

00:39:19.550 --> 00:39:24.050
were evolved with the frontier
being very near like that.

00:39:24.050 --> 00:39:27.650
So it takes a real
call to re-examine

00:39:27.650 --> 00:39:29.940
what's the processes?

00:39:29.940 --> 00:39:33.860
What are the processes by which
our organizations can adapt

00:39:33.860 --> 00:39:36.920
and start harnessing this
thing and our societies learn

00:39:36.920 --> 00:39:39.050
how to cope with it?

00:39:39.050 --> 00:39:43.750
So we need to look at different
processes for our evolving.

00:39:43.750 --> 00:39:46.660
And this has led me
to that bootstrapping.

00:39:46.660 --> 00:39:49.270
And then it leads to
things like oh, let's

00:39:49.270 --> 00:39:54.100
re-examine the infrastructure by
which our organizations improve

00:39:54.100 --> 00:39:58.240
and see if the improvement
infrastructure can be improved,

00:39:58.240 --> 00:39:59.980
and then if you
can bootstrap it.

00:39:59.980 --> 00:40:03.050
So that's the message.

00:40:03.050 --> 00:40:06.200
And it turned out that,
for the formulation

00:40:06.200 --> 00:40:10.660
we evolved, that one of the key
elements in that improvement

00:40:10.660 --> 00:40:13.600
infrastructure is what
we called an improvement

00:40:13.600 --> 00:40:17.500
community, something to
have special focus on.

00:40:17.500 --> 00:40:20.470
And LAI is an
improvement community.

00:40:20.470 --> 00:40:23.500
And then it's saying
that look, if we

00:40:23.500 --> 00:40:26.320
can bring the
modernization work,

00:40:26.320 --> 00:40:29.020
especially in collective
knowledge work,

00:40:29.020 --> 00:40:32.770
into appropriate
improvement communities,

00:40:32.770 --> 00:40:37.360
this is going to give us
an extra second degree

00:40:37.360 --> 00:40:38.890
acceleration or
something like that.

00:40:38.890 --> 00:40:41.640
It gives extra
strategic leverage

00:40:41.640 --> 00:40:43.510
in the improvement process.

00:40:43.510 --> 00:40:46.180
And so that's one of the reasons
why, when I talked to Earl,

00:40:46.180 --> 00:40:47.560
I thought, oh boy.

00:40:47.560 --> 00:40:52.090
There's a high tech
improvement community,

00:40:52.090 --> 00:40:56.290
and it's based in MIT, which
has a few people that are used

00:40:56.290 --> 00:40:59.300
to working with information.

00:40:59.300 --> 00:41:01.000
And it's a great, great model.

00:41:01.000 --> 00:41:02.980
So let's talk a little
more about that.

00:41:06.160 --> 00:41:10.060
So one of the strategic things
that somehow there have to be

00:41:10.060 --> 00:41:14.860
is ways to establish outposts
out in that frontier.

00:41:14.860 --> 00:41:19.330
And it isn't a matter of
going out there and setting up

00:41:19.330 --> 00:41:21.970
a group of people that,
by themselves, are just

00:41:21.970 --> 00:41:24.160
doing startling things.

00:41:24.160 --> 00:41:28.180
The way that that's going to
show organizations how to go

00:41:28.180 --> 00:41:31.270
is if those are doing real work.

00:41:31.270 --> 00:41:34.450
And it'd be very handy if
the real work they're doing

00:41:34.450 --> 00:41:38.950
is a visible part of the
work the organizations are

00:41:38.950 --> 00:41:40.910
doing already.

00:41:40.910 --> 00:41:42.820
So that means you
don't set them up

00:41:42.820 --> 00:41:46.200
in a laboratory and glass bowl
doing their special things.

00:41:46.200 --> 00:41:48.640
So there are a lot of
thinking about that.

00:41:48.640 --> 00:41:50.770
And how do you get the high
performance groups that

00:41:50.770 --> 00:41:51.954
are going to be doing that?

00:41:51.954 --> 00:41:52.870
How are they composed?

00:41:52.870 --> 00:41:53.953
What are they going to do?

00:41:53.953 --> 00:41:57.140
So all of that is an important
part of thinking this way.

00:41:57.140 --> 00:41:59.030
So we've talked
about this picture

00:41:59.030 --> 00:42:00.820
of the co-evolution frontier.

00:42:00.820 --> 00:42:03.760
And let's talk about, then,
one of the very important

00:42:03.760 --> 00:42:07.330
improvement categories
that's strategic in there,

00:42:07.330 --> 00:42:10.660
is the collective intelligence.

00:42:10.660 --> 00:42:13.480
So you said, you have
a bunch of people,

00:42:13.480 --> 00:42:17.050
and they're involved some
organization in this mandala

00:42:17.050 --> 00:42:21.370
kind of thing in here, with
all of the cross connections

00:42:21.370 --> 00:42:26.050
implying that every member
of this organization

00:42:26.050 --> 00:42:28.720
needs to have a channel
for communicating

00:42:28.720 --> 00:42:31.460
with every other one.

00:42:31.460 --> 00:42:34.030
And so that's the way
they work together.

00:42:34.030 --> 00:42:36.430
Then it's obvious
that if they're

00:42:36.430 --> 00:42:38.810
going to get
collectively smarter,

00:42:38.810 --> 00:42:40.990
they have to manage
the knowledge in there,

00:42:40.990 --> 00:42:44.620
and in the traditional
sense, treat a document

00:42:44.620 --> 00:42:49.000
as a container with some offered
knowledge in it like that.

00:42:49.000 --> 00:42:51.100
You said, well, it's
going to be documents.

00:42:51.100 --> 00:42:53.860
And with the way the web
has taught people like that,

00:42:53.860 --> 00:42:57.910
it's easy now to tell them
that the web is a beautiful way

00:42:57.910 --> 00:43:00.250
to start managing
their knowledge.

00:43:00.250 --> 00:43:04.150
And how do you go, then,
making them smarter?

00:43:04.150 --> 00:43:07.810
Well, that's something we could
talk hours and hours about,

00:43:07.810 --> 00:43:08.620
too.

00:43:08.620 --> 00:43:11.140
But the experience
and the motivations

00:43:11.140 --> 00:43:14.760
and the things we experimented
with just really show up a lot.

00:43:14.760 --> 00:43:20.770
And so it's how do you really
move beyond the knowledge work

00:43:20.770 --> 00:43:25.570
capabilities that the
vendors can offer you,

00:43:25.570 --> 00:43:32.320
because the user organizations
aren't yet proactively up ahead

00:43:32.320 --> 00:43:35.890
enough to say, I want to start
learning about the really

00:43:35.890 --> 00:43:39.310
far out things, rather
than the way it is now,

00:43:39.310 --> 00:43:41.050
that what we're
going to think about

00:43:41.050 --> 00:43:43.660
is what we can implement
today or tomorrow?

00:43:43.660 --> 00:43:46.960
And we have the political
problems of conflict

00:43:46.960 --> 00:43:48.400
inside our
organization of people

00:43:48.400 --> 00:43:50.290
that don't want to have
to change that much

00:43:50.290 --> 00:43:55.600
or are suspicious, all kinds
of what you end up realizing

00:43:55.600 --> 00:44:04.000
are very natural cultural
impedances in that.

00:44:04.000 --> 00:44:07.810
And so in the end, there's
going to be a dynamic knowledge

00:44:07.810 --> 00:44:12.820
repository that's electronic
hyper-media, linked like that.

00:44:12.820 --> 00:44:17.030
And the ways for
organizations to manage that,

00:44:17.030 --> 00:44:19.850
they want to be a lot more
alert about the outside world.

00:44:19.850 --> 00:44:23.230
So they're going to have to
be able to go out and actively

00:44:23.230 --> 00:44:26.860
ingest and assess and organize
intelligence about what

00:44:26.860 --> 00:44:29.320
the outside world is doing.

00:44:29.320 --> 00:44:32.410
And this social
organism that you

00:44:32.410 --> 00:44:37.510
call an organization is going
to be judged on its IQ a lot

00:44:37.510 --> 00:44:40.990
by, oh, how sensitive
and perceptual

00:44:40.990 --> 00:44:44.350
is it about what's going on in
the outside world that offers

00:44:44.350 --> 00:44:46.490
opportunities or threats?

00:44:46.490 --> 00:44:51.680
And when either of those
occurs, how smart and clever

00:44:51.680 --> 00:44:54.760
are their plan for how to cope?

00:44:54.760 --> 00:44:58.240
And how well do they, then,
marshal their resources

00:44:58.240 --> 00:45:00.730
and coordinate them in coping?

00:45:00.730 --> 00:45:04.090
And how quick are they
to adapt and shift?

00:45:04.090 --> 00:45:07.480
And you begin realizing the
way the future is going to go,

00:45:07.480 --> 00:45:11.050
that the annual yearly
budget thing is probably

00:45:11.050 --> 00:45:13.030
going to have to
go by the boards

00:45:13.030 --> 00:45:14.500
and become a much more dynamic.

00:45:14.500 --> 00:45:17.110
And a lot of things
are changing.

00:45:17.110 --> 00:45:20.170
The recorded dialogue
and the knowledge product

00:45:20.170 --> 00:45:24.220
are just there, a way to say,
oh, any given time you really

00:45:24.220 --> 00:45:27.545
want all the plans, the designs,
the current state of things,

00:45:27.545 --> 00:45:29.170
the current state of
your understanding

00:45:29.170 --> 00:45:31.750
about the outside world, the
current state of the scenarios

00:45:31.750 --> 00:45:35.390
that you're depending
upon, is all sort

00:45:35.390 --> 00:45:38.380
of what you call knowledge
products, available for people

00:45:38.380 --> 00:45:40.420
to use in there.

00:45:40.420 --> 00:45:42.670
And how they study
it how it's organized

00:45:42.670 --> 00:45:45.850
is some whole field
of exploration itself.

00:45:45.850 --> 00:45:48.100
And then at any
given time, there's

00:45:48.100 --> 00:45:49.660
a lot of dialogue about it.

00:45:49.660 --> 00:45:53.360
And you're in the midst of a
dialogue exercise right now,

00:45:53.360 --> 00:45:55.270
this morning.

00:45:55.270 --> 00:45:59.080
And recording that--
well, what I say

00:45:59.080 --> 00:46:01.030
and what I present
here like that,

00:46:01.030 --> 00:46:03.280
the technology is already
available for that

00:46:03.280 --> 00:46:06.040
to get nailed down
and be on a web,

00:46:06.040 --> 00:46:09.325
and automatically
transcribing my speech

00:46:09.325 --> 00:46:12.740
as much, well enough at least
so that you can scan for that.

00:46:12.740 --> 00:46:15.130
So you can go back
and then find out

00:46:15.130 --> 00:46:17.920
what I said and look at it
again, and say, oh yeah.

00:46:17.920 --> 00:46:18.740
That's interesting.

00:46:18.740 --> 00:46:21.100
And then the way you
can make comments

00:46:21.100 --> 00:46:23.800
on it are the links that
you'll be able to provide can

00:46:23.800 --> 00:46:26.560
point to a particular
passage that I spoke

00:46:26.560 --> 00:46:29.980
and its associated diagram,
or run up and down that.

00:46:29.980 --> 00:46:32.410
Or cleverly say,
hey, I want to hear

00:46:32.410 --> 00:46:36.130
what he says, and then show the
three kind of slide pictures

00:46:36.130 --> 00:46:38.260
that he showed
that went on that.

00:46:38.260 --> 00:46:40.330
And then I'd like, when
you follow that link,

00:46:40.330 --> 00:46:42.400
to highlight something
there, because that's

00:46:42.400 --> 00:46:44.110
what I'd like to talk about.

00:46:44.110 --> 00:46:46.240
Well, that's the
kind of link things

00:46:46.240 --> 00:46:48.370
that were part of our plan.

00:46:48.370 --> 00:46:51.400
And the basic
underlying standards

00:46:51.400 --> 00:46:53.860
for the extended markup
language and such

00:46:53.860 --> 00:46:56.200
will provide things like that.

00:46:56.200 --> 00:47:01.120
So then people make comments
on this and questions,

00:47:01.120 --> 00:47:04.480
and the dialogue's comment
on the comments grows.

00:47:04.480 --> 00:47:08.250
So these are all things that
technology can offer today.

00:47:08.250 --> 00:47:11.140
But it's just a
introductory example

00:47:11.140 --> 00:47:15.070
of this required dialogue
is a dynamic part

00:47:15.070 --> 00:47:18.940
of integrating intelligence and
arguing about what's there now,

00:47:18.940 --> 00:47:22.870
by which your knowledge
product evolves and changes.

00:47:22.870 --> 00:47:27.960
So every organization that
survives into very far

00:47:27.960 --> 00:47:32.410
in the next century is going to
have to start living like this.

00:47:32.410 --> 00:47:35.110
So there are a few other things
that you think about in this

00:47:35.110 --> 00:47:40.780
and say, oh, if I'm a
division or a part of a bigger

00:47:40.780 --> 00:47:44.470
organization, my division
or project or program

00:47:44.470 --> 00:47:47.680
is going to have its own
knowledge repository that's

00:47:47.680 --> 00:47:52.910
got to be dynamic and totally
relevant to what we're doing.

00:47:52.910 --> 00:47:55.600
And yet, the whole
organization does, too.

00:47:55.600 --> 00:47:57.670
And so every one of
these nodes around here

00:47:57.670 --> 00:48:03.490
has its own dynamic repository,
as well as the whole thing.

00:48:03.490 --> 00:48:07.210
And so all of those are
evolving concurrently,

00:48:07.210 --> 00:48:10.420
and yet need to stay in
sync, that none of them

00:48:10.420 --> 00:48:17.080
is looking at a future or
something that is in any way

00:48:17.080 --> 00:48:20.480
contrary to any of the others.

00:48:20.480 --> 00:48:24.800
So it's that concurrency thing
that really hits the demand,

00:48:24.800 --> 00:48:26.467
yet you don't hear
that very often.

00:48:26.467 --> 00:48:28.300
You know they have to
have interoperability,

00:48:28.300 --> 00:48:29.870
but not scaling.

00:48:29.870 --> 00:48:31.990
So the challenge is large.

00:48:31.990 --> 00:48:33.760
And the challenge
there is something

00:48:33.760 --> 00:48:37.450
that you don't just get by
getting a new product out

00:48:37.450 --> 00:48:39.350
by a given vendor or something.

00:48:39.350 --> 00:48:42.180
It's got to be
something that evolves

00:48:42.180 --> 00:48:45.110
in a very different way.

00:48:45.110 --> 00:48:48.520
And so we ended up with
this acronym CoDIAK,

00:48:48.520 --> 00:48:52.420
saying that that describes
a core set of capabilities

00:48:52.420 --> 00:48:55.110
in the future
smart organization,

00:48:55.110 --> 00:48:58.744
that they concurrently develop,
integrate, and apply knowledge.

00:48:58.744 --> 00:49:00.160
It's the best we
could sort of do.

00:49:00.160 --> 00:49:02.800
So we use that term
a lot, and say,

00:49:02.800 --> 00:49:05.140
how do you go about
improving that?

00:49:05.140 --> 00:49:09.250
And you can just guess
that every one, almost

00:49:09.250 --> 00:49:13.330
every objective you'd like to
set up for what LAI can do,

00:49:13.330 --> 00:49:15.540
can do a lot better if the
organizations were better

00:49:15.540 --> 00:49:16.510
at this.

00:49:16.510 --> 00:49:19.390
And a very important
thing, oh, LAI

00:49:19.390 --> 00:49:21.850
could operate a lot
more effectively.

00:49:21.850 --> 00:49:25.150
So where should you put your
initial exploratory energy

00:49:25.150 --> 00:49:28.585
about doing outposts that are
trying to learn how to do this?

00:49:28.585 --> 00:49:31.450
Oh, strategically
speaking, LAI is the place

00:49:31.450 --> 00:49:35.720
to start investing in learning
advanced how to do this.

00:49:35.720 --> 00:49:39.680
So I got some great lessons
interacting with the McDonnell

00:49:39.680 --> 00:49:40.520
Douglas people.

00:49:40.520 --> 00:49:42.520
So they were showing
me that well,

00:49:42.520 --> 00:49:44.710
so you have a couple
thousand people working

00:49:44.710 --> 00:49:47.710
inside a McDonnell plant
for on a fighter plane.

00:49:47.710 --> 00:49:50.530
But look at what you have
in these tiers of suppliers

00:49:50.530 --> 00:49:53.580
and the kind of interactions
and design coordination

00:49:53.580 --> 00:49:54.950
that have to go on there.

00:49:54.950 --> 00:49:58.060
So there is a real
example for concurrency

00:49:58.060 --> 00:50:00.730
and interoperability
requirement.

00:50:00.730 --> 00:50:04.030
So these are things that
you very rarely, very rarely

00:50:04.030 --> 00:50:07.820
hear about when vendors
are selling you things.

00:50:07.820 --> 00:50:12.090
But this is the
real marketplace.

00:50:15.680 --> 00:50:18.830
So anyway, this open
hyper-document system

00:50:18.830 --> 00:50:22.100
is something that
evolved in our minds

00:50:22.100 --> 00:50:24.380
like 14 years ago at
McDonnell Douglas,

00:50:24.380 --> 00:50:27.260
about saying you
really, really have

00:50:27.260 --> 00:50:31.010
to come out with a standard,
open, hyper-document system.

00:50:31.010 --> 00:50:33.620
And the HTML offered
a beautiful way

00:50:33.620 --> 00:50:35.930
to get a standard
way for publishing.

00:50:35.930 --> 00:50:38.450
And the XML is
starting to advance it.

00:50:38.450 --> 00:50:41.030
But most of that is
being done by vendors,

00:50:41.030 --> 00:50:43.070
the evolution of standards.

00:50:43.070 --> 00:50:45.920
And what is critically
needed, really,

00:50:45.920 --> 00:50:49.640
is the proactive
involvement of the end user

00:50:49.640 --> 00:50:53.180
organizations in the
evolution of the standards

00:50:53.180 --> 00:50:54.350
for this thing.

00:50:54.350 --> 00:50:57.340
So these are sort
of the properties

00:50:57.340 --> 00:50:58.490
that all these things--

00:51:05.260 --> 00:51:08.000
My watch ran out of battery.

00:51:08.000 --> 00:51:13.390
So you can just tell how well
I'm connected to today's world.

00:51:13.390 --> 00:51:14.670
So I had to borrow Earl's.

00:51:14.670 --> 00:51:18.580
So this means he can't tell
me when my time is done.

00:51:18.580 --> 00:51:20.914
And that was a very
clever tactical thing,

00:51:20.914 --> 00:51:21.580
don't you think?

00:51:25.450 --> 00:51:27.460
I should have borrowed
everybody's watch there

00:51:27.460 --> 00:51:28.690
so he would have no way.

00:51:32.200 --> 00:51:38.320
Anyway, here again, it's
that sort of strategic need

00:51:38.320 --> 00:51:41.020
to get the end
user organization,

00:51:41.020 --> 00:51:44.890
or the real customer
is the organization,

00:51:44.890 --> 00:51:49.180
in the IT world that's coming
up, and that's here now.

00:51:49.180 --> 00:51:54.310
So how do your organizations
move into this space?

00:51:54.310 --> 00:51:57.010
And it's only going to
happen if there becomes

00:51:57.010 --> 00:52:00.100
a real standard for
the knowledge packages,

00:52:00.100 --> 00:52:03.340
that it's not a
standard set by a vendor

00:52:03.340 --> 00:52:06.190
that this is my kind
of file, or something.

00:52:06.190 --> 00:52:08.040
So there's a standard file.

00:52:08.040 --> 00:52:11.230
And not only that,
there's standard sets

00:52:11.230 --> 00:52:15.430
of functional capabilities that
can be applied to those things.

00:52:15.430 --> 00:52:17.410
And with a standard
file there, there

00:52:17.410 --> 00:52:22.360
are a whole array of as yet
unexplored, underutilized

00:52:22.360 --> 00:52:26.890
properties that can be
embedded inside the document.

00:52:26.890 --> 00:52:31.600
And some that we built in
ours, for instance, were--

00:52:31.600 --> 00:52:34.980
it was just early
experiments with it--

00:52:34.980 --> 00:52:40.120
that the identity and the
date, time, second stamp

00:52:40.120 --> 00:52:47.034
on every paragraph or line of
code was the last edited time.

00:52:47.034 --> 00:52:48.700
And this was really
interesting, the way

00:52:48.700 --> 00:52:50.680
you could employ that property.

00:52:50.680 --> 00:52:52.690
It's like, oh.

00:52:52.690 --> 00:52:55.730
There's some problems
in-- our source code

00:52:55.730 --> 00:53:01.120
has all been hyperlinked
from the late '60s on.

00:53:01.120 --> 00:53:04.210
So 500,000 lines of it, that you
could just jump to any place.

00:53:04.210 --> 00:53:07.840
And our email was
hyperlinked from 1970 on.

00:53:07.840 --> 00:53:10.780
So it could point to any place
in the code or anything else,

00:53:10.780 --> 00:53:14.010
and you could also point
to any passage in an email.

00:53:14.010 --> 00:53:15.980
And it was just living that way.

00:53:18.490 --> 00:53:20.470
So there are new
bugs in the software?

00:53:20.470 --> 00:53:24.940
Oh, well, they're probably
in this section of software.

00:53:24.940 --> 00:53:26.830
Well, one of the
things we said is

00:53:26.830 --> 00:53:29.470
every time you
jump on something,

00:53:29.470 --> 00:53:32.610
your link can specify what view.

00:53:32.610 --> 00:53:35.560
And it can say, just show me the
first line of every paragraph,

00:53:35.560 --> 00:53:37.060
or if it's
hierarchically organized,

00:53:37.060 --> 00:53:39.280
just the first two levels
or something like this.

00:53:39.280 --> 00:53:41.590
Or here's a content
filter to put on.

00:53:41.590 --> 00:53:44.200
And only show me those
paragraphs or lines

00:53:44.200 --> 00:53:47.680
that have a certain
content or were

00:53:47.680 --> 00:53:49.940
changed within a given period.

00:53:49.940 --> 00:53:52.710
So there's some new bug
is in there like that?

00:53:52.710 --> 00:53:54.085
Well, just show
me something that

00:53:54.085 --> 00:53:55.660
was changed in the last month.

00:53:55.660 --> 00:53:57.400
And you'll see five
or three or four

00:53:57.400 --> 00:53:59.080
people that have been changing.

00:53:59.080 --> 00:54:03.040
And then one that always
tickles me is, oh, Fred.

00:54:03.040 --> 00:54:04.750
He's always the problem.

00:54:04.750 --> 00:54:08.870
Just show me everything Fred has
changed, and I'll find the bug.

00:54:08.870 --> 00:54:11.830
And so we had all these
kind of ways to live.

00:54:11.830 --> 00:54:16.210
And it's learning how to
implant the kind of properties

00:54:16.210 --> 00:54:20.840
that give you extended
capabilities beyond the space.

00:54:20.840 --> 00:54:24.640
And a document
now is not a model

00:54:24.640 --> 00:54:29.120
of a paper document at
all, with its rigid thing

00:54:29.120 --> 00:54:31.210
that's only you can scroll.

00:54:31.210 --> 00:54:33.340
You know, you can
jump very flexibly,

00:54:33.340 --> 00:54:35.230
have all kinds of viewing
options and things

00:54:35.230 --> 00:54:37.150
that you learn how to apply.

00:54:37.150 --> 00:54:39.220
So it's inevitable
that that comes.

00:54:39.220 --> 00:54:43.120
But that won't be
interoperably available

00:54:43.120 --> 00:54:45.430
unless there gets to be
a standard way in which

00:54:45.430 --> 00:54:46.690
the document is expressed.

00:54:46.690 --> 00:54:49.630
So there's a whole set of
standards out there that are--

00:54:49.630 --> 00:54:51.610
and the standards
working group--

00:54:51.610 --> 00:54:53.140
that are available
for evolution.

00:54:53.140 --> 00:54:57.550
But it's just
absolutely imperative

00:54:57.550 --> 00:55:01.000
that end user organizations
get proactively involved.

00:55:01.000 --> 00:55:04.630
And one way to do it is
through consortia like this.

00:55:04.630 --> 00:55:08.320
And we'll show you
more like that.

00:55:08.320 --> 00:55:11.920
So we actually have funding
now to proceed on this.

00:55:11.920 --> 00:55:14.940
When I say we now, it's--

00:55:14.940 --> 00:55:19.360
this whole picture about what's
needed in proactive end user

00:55:19.360 --> 00:55:21.670
organizations
emerged so strongly

00:55:21.670 --> 00:55:24.940
in the late '80s for me when
I was at McDonnell Douglas.

00:55:24.940 --> 00:55:28.310
And not to point
at them as anybody

00:55:28.310 --> 00:55:30.910
who was particularly reactive.

00:55:30.910 --> 00:55:34.090
Every organization had the
same kind of reactions.

00:55:34.090 --> 00:55:37.990
But we demonstrated what
you could do in things.

00:55:37.990 --> 00:55:42.200
And we're showing how you can
make a link in the requirements

00:55:42.200 --> 00:55:46.210
or a project status
report, and a link

00:55:46.210 --> 00:55:48.850
pointing to something
in a CAD system

00:55:48.850 --> 00:55:51.310
that when it got
to the CAD system,

00:55:51.310 --> 00:55:54.670
you got the protocols
arranged, so it would evoke

00:55:54.670 --> 00:55:57.640
the right kind of macro
that would produce a given

00:55:57.640 --> 00:56:01.330
view and scale and view of a
certain thing and highlight it,

00:56:01.330 --> 00:56:05.830
so that that's the way that the
user, in seeing this comment

00:56:05.830 --> 00:56:07.930
on it, could go look.

00:56:07.930 --> 00:56:10.827
Or in the CAD
environment like that,

00:56:10.827 --> 00:56:12.910
they're talking about the
plating or the finishing

00:56:12.910 --> 00:56:14.500
or something like
that, and they could

00:56:14.500 --> 00:56:17.110
have in a comment sort
of sense, a link that

00:56:17.110 --> 00:56:21.800
go phew, right to the actual
specifications of those things.

00:56:21.800 --> 00:56:24.160
So all those things
were just there

00:56:24.160 --> 00:56:25.820
to add to what we could do.

00:56:25.820 --> 00:56:33.700
And it ended up in that the
poor head of corporate CIO

00:56:33.700 --> 00:56:38.110
was really between a rock and
a hard place, because he says,

00:56:38.110 --> 00:56:40.910
well Deck and Hewlett-Packard
and IBM, none of them

00:56:40.910 --> 00:56:42.420
are talking about this.

00:56:42.420 --> 00:56:44.110
Course the language
he used at the time

00:56:44.110 --> 00:56:45.880
was a little more
colorful than that.

00:56:45.880 --> 00:56:49.160
But he says, I'm not going
to go ahead and do something

00:56:49.160 --> 00:56:51.160
where out there, they
don't even recognize,

00:56:51.160 --> 00:56:53.380
they don't even
know what a link is.

00:56:53.380 --> 00:56:57.990
So there's just
a question there,

00:56:57.990 --> 00:57:00.630
like that the world
was not ready.

00:57:00.630 --> 00:57:04.320
And you can't go point a finger
at any particular person.

00:57:04.320 --> 00:57:06.390
The world just wasn't
ready, for some reason

00:57:06.390 --> 00:57:09.660
that the evolution of
the thinking in scenarios

00:57:09.660 --> 00:57:15.660
about the future, about the
inevitable much more capacity

00:57:15.660 --> 00:57:19.710
and bandwidth per dollar
that was just inevitable,

00:57:19.710 --> 00:57:21.540
that changing the
organization is

00:57:21.540 --> 00:57:26.130
going to take a lot longer than
the change in that technology.

00:57:26.130 --> 00:57:32.100
So anyway one of my daughters
who had been working with this

00:57:32.100 --> 00:57:35.250
area-- and also, she and her
husband had gotten experience

00:57:35.250 --> 00:57:38.050
with a startup--

00:57:38.050 --> 00:57:41.580
we set up this Bootstrap
Institute 10 years ago

00:57:41.580 --> 00:57:44.640
to say, let's raise a flag
and start telling the world.

00:57:44.640 --> 00:57:48.100
And it's been a very
interesting experience.

00:57:48.100 --> 00:57:51.540
But the momentum is slowly
picking up and accelerating now

00:57:51.540 --> 00:57:54.150
that there is a
Bootstrap Alliance that's

00:57:54.150 --> 00:57:56.650
started, that says, hey.

00:57:56.650 --> 00:57:59.970
Let's get strategically
set up so that people

00:57:59.970 --> 00:58:02.050
can participate in this.

00:58:02.050 --> 00:58:05.970
And last November,
by George, Japan

00:58:05.970 --> 00:58:09.480
set up a Japanese chapter
of the Bootstrap Alliance,

00:58:09.480 --> 00:58:12.810
because one of the things we
were talking about is, hey,

00:58:12.810 --> 00:58:14.580
this is a scalable thing.

00:58:14.580 --> 00:58:17.430
It's got to be, to have a
infrastructure that really will

00:58:17.430 --> 00:58:19.980
work like this, it
needs to be scalable,

00:58:19.980 --> 00:58:24.050
and to be scalable to
national or global.

00:58:24.050 --> 00:58:28.290
And it'd be worthwhile to
set up a national improvement

00:58:28.290 --> 00:58:30.330
infrastructure.

00:58:30.330 --> 00:58:33.285
Well, the Japanese
aren't quite exactly

00:58:33.285 --> 00:58:34.660
saying that's what
they're doing,

00:58:34.660 --> 00:58:36.659
but they're setting up
their own chapter of that

00:58:36.659 --> 00:58:38.460
to be participating in that.

00:58:38.460 --> 00:58:41.380
And so now we've come
back home and say, well,

00:58:41.380 --> 00:58:44.345
let's get the US more proactive.

00:58:44.345 --> 00:58:46.720
And we're getting a few other
leads from other countries.

00:58:46.720 --> 00:58:50.370
But the whole idea
of really paying

00:58:50.370 --> 00:58:53.010
more attention to the
improvement infrastructure

00:58:53.010 --> 00:58:57.000
and how it can be improved
and the strategies

00:58:57.000 --> 00:59:00.280
for investing in it--

00:59:00.280 --> 00:59:02.040
so anyway, collective IQ.

00:59:02.040 --> 00:59:04.770
ABCs of improvement
is the next one.

00:59:04.770 --> 00:59:11.680
And so this is condensing
an abstracting quite a bit,

00:59:11.680 --> 00:59:17.270
but go look at any one of your
business organizations in LAI.

00:59:17.270 --> 00:59:22.340
And just look at these three
categories of activity.

00:59:22.340 --> 00:59:26.110
So don't say the categories
of people or something

00:59:26.110 --> 00:59:27.820
at the outset.

00:59:27.820 --> 00:59:32.050
But the A category
is everything the way

00:59:32.050 --> 00:59:36.040
the business is done today,
every operational activity

00:59:36.040 --> 00:59:39.160
that conducting in the business
day, which includes hiring

00:59:39.160 --> 00:59:41.830
new people and training them
to do the work that you're

00:59:41.830 --> 00:59:43.550
doing today.

00:59:43.550 --> 00:59:46.990
So then any activity that's
improving the capability

00:59:46.990 --> 00:59:52.930
to do your A activity, just
categorize that as a B.

00:59:52.930 --> 00:59:54.910
And very often,
that's somebody that

00:59:54.910 --> 00:59:58.300
wears an A hat many
hours a day, but on B, it

00:59:58.300 --> 01:00:01.240
goes into a committee that's
talking about something

01:00:01.240 --> 01:00:02.980
changing or something,
or planning,

01:00:02.980 --> 01:00:06.500
or actually implementing it, or
some budgeted change project.

01:00:06.500 --> 01:00:08.770
And the change
agents often spend

01:00:08.770 --> 01:00:11.950
most of their time in there.

01:00:11.950 --> 01:00:16.180
Except then there's this other
category that became important

01:00:16.180 --> 01:00:17.640
as we were thinking about it.

01:00:17.640 --> 01:00:21.460
It's what activity is there
to improve the capability

01:00:21.460 --> 01:00:24.640
to do the B, to do the changing?

01:00:24.640 --> 01:00:28.360
And so in a real
sense, all of you

01:00:28.360 --> 01:00:33.850
that are from organizations
here today, have your C hat on.

01:00:33.850 --> 01:00:36.010
And we found a lot of
very interesting things

01:00:36.010 --> 01:00:39.620
that start evolving when you're
thinking this way, which again,

01:00:39.620 --> 01:00:41.890
you know, you could dig
deeper into dialogue,

01:00:41.890 --> 01:00:44.260
and we could be here half
a day or more on that.

01:00:46.810 --> 01:00:49.220
So this was very important.

01:00:49.220 --> 01:00:53.830
And one of the big things is,
when the frontier is exploding,

01:00:53.830 --> 01:00:56.110
B and C become a
lot more important.

01:00:56.110 --> 01:00:58.390
And C, which hardly
gets any recognition

01:00:58.390 --> 01:01:00.970
at all today in any
corporation or something,

01:01:00.970 --> 01:01:02.630
becomes much more important.

01:01:02.630 --> 01:01:04.960
So let's talk about
that a little bit.

01:01:04.960 --> 01:01:08.470
It's sort of like,
say, that all right.

01:01:08.470 --> 01:01:10.240
We've got a frontier
out there that we're

01:01:10.240 --> 01:01:12.040
going to try to go after.

01:01:12.040 --> 01:01:17.350
And the C is sort
of like the scout.

01:01:17.350 --> 01:01:19.450
It's sort of, in order
to improve your ability

01:01:19.450 --> 01:01:22.434
to improve, which
here would mean,

01:01:22.434 --> 01:01:24.850
in order to improve your ability
to get out there and move

01:01:24.850 --> 01:01:27.610
your organization, you
need to scout it, find out

01:01:27.610 --> 01:01:31.720
what's out there, and what would
be the different trails that

01:01:31.720 --> 01:01:37.340
are available, and how you
can sort of teach the B kinds.

01:01:37.340 --> 01:01:40.950
And you says, oh, the B
is the guy that's got to--

01:01:40.950 --> 01:01:43.190
he's the wagon train
master or something.

01:01:43.190 --> 01:01:45.930
He's got to move you out there.

01:01:45.930 --> 01:01:51.250
And so, in the business of
talking to people about being

01:01:51.250 --> 01:01:56.470
change agents, both of them
are change agents, B and C.

01:01:56.470 --> 01:01:59.770
But it becomes important
to clarify when

01:01:59.770 --> 01:02:03.219
you're in a B role or a C role.

01:02:03.219 --> 01:02:05.260
And then it'd be very
important for organizations

01:02:05.260 --> 01:02:08.020
to start trying to do their
auditing and something

01:02:08.020 --> 01:02:11.320
so that they could tell you how
much money they're spending,

01:02:11.320 --> 01:02:13.540
what the budgets
are for those two,

01:02:13.540 --> 01:02:16.335
because just like budgets
for product R and D

01:02:16.335 --> 01:02:19.840
or something, where you
sort of, by the time

01:02:19.840 --> 01:02:21.700
you're setting your
budgets, you sort of

01:02:21.700 --> 01:02:23.710
take stock of what the
rest of your industry

01:02:23.710 --> 01:02:27.220
does like that, because if
you're too far off from them,

01:02:27.220 --> 01:02:28.810
you're either behind
the ball or you

01:02:28.810 --> 01:02:30.080
might be overspending or what.

01:02:33.880 --> 01:02:37.990
But anyway, this is
something we haven't

01:02:37.990 --> 01:02:42.670
found any organization that
even has an auditing set up

01:02:42.670 --> 01:02:46.870
where they can classify and
tell you what that C is.

01:02:49.450 --> 01:02:55.910
And yet, the thing about
this explosive frontier--

01:02:55.910 --> 01:02:56.410
thank you.

01:03:08.820 --> 01:03:11.712
So anyway, every
one of these steps

01:03:11.712 --> 01:03:13.170
we're taking through
here like that

01:03:13.170 --> 01:03:15.510
are things that there are
a lot more digging into.

01:03:15.510 --> 01:03:20.670
One can get-- and what I
would really secretly like

01:03:20.670 --> 01:03:26.370
is to get LAI as an
organization interested

01:03:26.370 --> 01:03:29.280
in this whole
strategy, and start

01:03:29.280 --> 01:03:33.490
knocking on the door of
our alliance to say, hey.

01:03:33.490 --> 01:03:38.370
We'd like to participate,
because the alliance

01:03:38.370 --> 01:03:39.990
is trying to--

01:03:39.990 --> 01:03:44.880
its job is to help different
improvement communities do

01:03:44.880 --> 01:03:47.730
their job better.

01:03:47.730 --> 01:03:52.760
So anyway, this is sort of the
objective for organizations

01:03:52.760 --> 01:03:53.770
frontier perspective.

01:03:53.770 --> 01:03:57.690
And it's just a part of the
improvement infrastructure

01:03:57.690 --> 01:03:58.650
we're talking about.

01:03:58.650 --> 01:04:01.350
So the next step we
can go to is talking

01:04:01.350 --> 01:04:02.760
about improvement communities.

01:04:05.370 --> 01:04:09.930
And here, you can
almost use LAI group.

01:04:09.930 --> 01:04:12.665
So among your
manufacturing organizations

01:04:12.665 --> 01:04:17.370
that are joining here, and
even some of the agencies,

01:04:17.370 --> 01:04:19.320
you can sort of look
up and down the street

01:04:19.320 --> 01:04:21.690
and pick out some
of them that have

01:04:21.690 --> 01:04:23.850
a similar set of
capabilities that they're

01:04:23.850 --> 01:04:26.800
interested in improving.

01:04:26.800 --> 01:04:33.240
And so in this case, the lean
aerospace activity capability.

01:04:33.240 --> 01:04:34.305
So you'd say, all right.

01:04:34.305 --> 01:04:35.430
We're doing that in common.

01:04:35.430 --> 01:04:39.310
So our Bs have similar
kind of things to do.

01:04:39.310 --> 01:04:41.580
And sometimes we'd
like to be a little bit

01:04:41.580 --> 01:04:44.530
proprietary about what
you actually do in A.

01:04:44.530 --> 01:04:47.540
So some of the B things,
we would just as soon keep

01:04:47.540 --> 01:04:49.800
on our belt. But wait a minute.

01:04:49.800 --> 01:04:52.080
How about the C?

01:04:52.080 --> 01:04:55.050
Well, suddenly that frontier
is going way out there.

01:04:55.050 --> 01:04:58.410
And we have to invest a
lot more in C in order

01:04:58.410 --> 01:05:03.310
to be able to know how we're
going to move our organization.

01:05:03.310 --> 01:05:07.950
So it really benefits us
to invest collectively.

01:05:07.950 --> 01:05:12.150
And that's just basically
why you're investing

01:05:12.150 --> 01:05:14.370
and participating in LAI.

01:05:14.370 --> 01:05:16.380
It's exploring and
finding out what's

01:05:16.380 --> 01:05:19.860
out there to do and to change
so that you can decide inside

01:05:19.860 --> 01:05:21.180
how to change and do it.

01:05:21.180 --> 01:05:24.260
So he says, really awkward.

01:05:36.750 --> 01:05:38.940
So we just said, oh.

01:05:38.940 --> 01:05:43.990
So what LAI essentially
is is a C community.

01:05:43.990 --> 01:05:46.380
You're getting together
in a community sense

01:05:46.380 --> 01:05:50.130
to do common work on
C. And so you say,

01:05:50.130 --> 01:05:51.539
that's really very important.

01:05:51.539 --> 01:05:53.580
So out in the world, there
are a lot of consortia

01:05:53.580 --> 01:05:57.800
that are doing that, and other
kind of improvement communities

01:05:57.800 --> 01:06:00.720
that don't call themselves
consortia, or call themselves

01:06:00.720 --> 01:06:06.619
a professional society
or a trade association.

01:06:06.619 --> 01:06:07.410
They're doing that.

01:06:07.410 --> 01:06:12.000
So these all have a
special sort of potential

01:06:12.000 --> 01:06:16.020
in boosting the rest of
society's capabilities.

01:06:16.020 --> 01:06:20.860
So this is just the
description of it.

01:06:20.860 --> 01:06:24.450
So one of the things we
talk about is saying, oh.

01:06:24.450 --> 01:06:28.320
We talk about what if the
improvement community decides

01:06:28.320 --> 01:06:34.110
it's going to really be a
proactive evolutionary pusher

01:06:34.110 --> 01:06:38.850
towards having this
collective IQ of itself

01:06:38.850 --> 01:06:40.500
a lot more effective?

01:06:40.500 --> 01:06:43.080
Not waiting till all these
member organizations do,

01:06:43.080 --> 01:06:47.490
but saying, oh, if we started
doing it ourselves, more

01:06:47.490 --> 01:06:50.010
aggressively, proactively,
really learning

01:06:50.010 --> 01:06:51.630
how to do the
collective knowledge

01:06:51.630 --> 01:06:54.960
work of the
consortium, then we're

01:06:54.960 --> 01:06:59.340
doing a lot better job
there, and the whole pursuit

01:06:59.340 --> 01:07:02.370
of how our members
can improve will

01:07:02.370 --> 01:07:04.590
be facilitated and improved.

01:07:04.590 --> 01:07:07.680
But also, the very
experience that they all

01:07:07.680 --> 01:07:12.210
can share by having this thing
be as advanced as possible

01:07:12.210 --> 01:07:14.880
is a real enlightenment
about what they can take back

01:07:14.880 --> 01:07:17.130
inside their organizations.

01:07:17.130 --> 01:07:20.130
And very soon, in
LAI, that would

01:07:20.130 --> 01:07:22.380
become one of its capabilities.

01:07:22.380 --> 01:07:25.830
It's interested in transferring
into the organizations, too.

01:07:25.830 --> 01:07:28.830
Essentially, how
you do this CoDIAK--

01:07:28.830 --> 01:07:31.200
Concurrently Develop
Integrate Apply Knowledge--

01:07:31.200 --> 01:07:33.060
in a better, improved
manner, would

01:07:33.060 --> 01:07:35.310
benefit every one of
the organizations that's

01:07:35.310 --> 01:07:36.040
a member of that.

01:07:36.040 --> 01:07:39.710
So that could become a
directed sort of pursuit of one

01:07:39.710 --> 01:07:43.230
of the capabilities, but
also be a front runner.

01:07:43.230 --> 01:07:45.570
And it's not just the
knowledge that that

01:07:45.570 --> 01:07:49.320
can collect and be in that
dynamic knowledge repository.

01:07:49.320 --> 01:07:54.500
It's the experience because
how things can work in there.

01:07:54.500 --> 01:07:59.350
And so that becomes
very important.

01:07:59.350 --> 01:08:01.830
So we talk about the
improvement vector,

01:08:01.830 --> 01:08:06.540
the vector of capabilities
that an improvement community

01:08:06.540 --> 01:08:09.390
is out after
collectively improving.

01:08:09.390 --> 01:08:11.490
And then we talk
about if they are

01:08:11.490 --> 01:08:14.100
using this kind
of advanced stuff

01:08:14.100 --> 01:08:16.380
in a really proactive
improving way,

01:08:16.380 --> 01:08:18.450
we've been calling them NICs--

01:08:18.450 --> 01:08:20.360
Network Improvement Communities.

01:08:24.029 --> 01:08:26.819
So that an improvement
community that's not actively

01:08:26.819 --> 01:08:32.729
doing that is just IC,
and some of our members

01:08:32.729 --> 01:08:35.580
have fun talking
about how they're just

01:08:35.580 --> 01:08:39.390
an IC, trying to be
derogatory, when you really

01:08:39.390 --> 01:08:41.200
could become a NIC.

01:08:41.200 --> 01:08:45.300
So anyway, this is
the idea that LAI

01:08:45.300 --> 01:08:47.010
is a real candidate for that.

01:08:47.010 --> 01:08:48.731
And so Earl was
telling me that plans

01:08:48.731 --> 01:08:50.189
that you've been
working on anyway,

01:08:50.189 --> 01:08:51.640
and you've already
got a website.

01:08:51.640 --> 01:08:53.430
But he says, oh,
like extensions--

01:08:53.430 --> 01:08:56.020
so these presentations
going on--

01:08:56.020 --> 01:08:58.830
and then the dialogue
about them, and much more,

01:08:58.830 --> 01:09:00.149
can start being in there.

01:09:00.149 --> 01:09:02.640
And much more of
the practice for how

01:09:02.640 --> 01:09:06.120
that begins to be an
important part of the way you

01:09:06.120 --> 01:09:07.229
participate--

01:09:07.229 --> 01:09:11.399
so it isn't just
LAI central staff.

01:09:11.399 --> 01:09:15.450
It's LAI participants
that get involved and have

01:09:15.450 --> 01:09:17.700
to expend energy
in participating

01:09:17.700 --> 01:09:18.750
in that evolution.

01:09:18.750 --> 01:09:22.260
And the way it works,
and you says, well maybe

01:09:22.260 --> 01:09:24.510
LAI's benefits aren't
going to be worth it.

01:09:24.510 --> 01:09:26.700
So you says, well,
just look at what

01:09:26.700 --> 01:09:30.899
you're learning about
how advanced knowledge

01:09:30.899 --> 01:09:32.609
work can be done.

01:09:32.609 --> 01:09:34.470
And that's a real
pilot operation

01:09:34.470 --> 01:09:37.620
for what you could bring back
inside your organization.

01:09:37.620 --> 01:09:41.220
And LAI's role can pretty soon
be there to facilitate that,

01:09:41.220 --> 01:09:42.899
too.

01:09:42.899 --> 01:09:44.819
So it can be a multiple winner.

01:09:50.520 --> 01:09:54.210
So anyway, this is
the sort of the thing.

01:09:54.210 --> 01:09:56.730
So you consider the payoff
to the aerospace industry

01:09:56.730 --> 01:10:01.480
if LAI converted itself into a
NIC, and not just a web user,

01:10:01.480 --> 01:10:04.980
but an example of a distributed
organization, actively

01:10:04.980 --> 01:10:07.590
and persistently improving
its CoDIAK capability

01:10:07.590 --> 01:10:09.390
and collective IQ.

01:10:09.390 --> 01:10:14.700
So this is sort of a
polite challenge, people.

01:10:14.700 --> 01:10:18.900
And LAI would become
more effective,

01:10:18.900 --> 01:10:22.920
improving its members critical
capabilities, and incidentally,

01:10:22.920 --> 01:10:26.100
early and direct boosting of
the members' collective IQ

01:10:26.100 --> 01:10:29.530
for the [INAUDIBLE] like that.

01:10:29.530 --> 01:10:32.780
So here's the idea for
the Bootstrap Alliance,

01:10:32.780 --> 01:10:35.600
that's now going
into its second year.

01:10:35.600 --> 01:10:38.670
It's saying, look, what are
the member organizations in it?

01:10:38.670 --> 01:10:42.810
Oh, the idea is
that they are NICs,

01:10:42.810 --> 01:10:49.710
and that the common improvement
vector that this NIC is there

01:10:49.710 --> 01:10:53.370
to do is to improve the
capability of its members

01:10:53.370 --> 01:10:55.660
to be better NICs.

01:10:55.660 --> 01:10:56.770
So that's the way it is.

01:10:56.770 --> 01:10:59.340
So there's some real
potential in that

01:10:59.340 --> 01:11:03.180
because it isn't something
you do just naturally.

01:11:03.180 --> 01:11:08.040
There's sort of no one product,
no one team or something

01:11:08.040 --> 01:11:13.410
that's going to produce to the
winning capability in there.

01:11:13.410 --> 01:11:14.580
It's got to be an evolution.

01:11:14.580 --> 01:11:17.310
So every one of
these NICs should

01:11:17.310 --> 01:11:21.040
be free to start learning
how, doing it itself.

01:11:21.040 --> 01:11:23.250
And this is the place
within the alliance

01:11:23.250 --> 01:11:25.750
to start assessing, analyzing.

01:11:25.750 --> 01:11:29.790
It's just like no
one outfit in LAI

01:11:29.790 --> 01:11:33.570
is, in competing, going to
design the end [INAUDIBLE]

01:11:33.570 --> 01:11:38.040
best lean system.

01:11:38.040 --> 01:11:39.720
There's a lot of
evolution going on

01:11:39.720 --> 01:11:42.030
in how you get the best case
studies and the assessment

01:11:42.030 --> 01:11:45.210
of it that you integrate in here
so that everybody can watch.

01:11:45.210 --> 01:11:48.240
You make a better environment
for all of the members

01:11:48.240 --> 01:11:50.610
to evolve in.

01:11:50.610 --> 01:11:59.290
And so this is briefly about
the improvement community.

01:11:59.290 --> 01:12:03.600
But then there is the strategy
about how you can really

01:12:03.600 --> 01:12:07.890
get maximum leverage out of that
in this bootstrapping sense.

01:12:07.890 --> 01:12:14.160
So that's sort of saying, if
your Bootstrap Alliance is

01:12:14.160 --> 01:12:18.120
careful, that early
on, it looks at what

01:12:18.120 --> 01:12:21.240
we call the bootstrapping
index for our NIC.

01:12:21.240 --> 01:12:24.525
That is, how much
are the capabilities

01:12:24.525 --> 01:12:28.350
that that NIC is trying to
improve among its members

01:12:28.350 --> 01:12:31.720
are capabilities that, brought
back into the alliance,

01:12:31.720 --> 01:12:33.960
will help be something
you would distribute

01:12:33.960 --> 01:12:38.050
in every NIC would need?

01:12:38.050 --> 01:12:41.320
And so we just find
some real value in that.

01:12:41.320 --> 01:12:48.750
So you know, like LAI over here,
it's not its dominant pursuit.

01:12:48.750 --> 01:12:51.040
But it's going to have
good ideas to share.

01:12:53.920 --> 01:12:56.160
But there are some
communities that we've

01:12:56.160 --> 01:12:59.640
been knocking on
the door of that I

01:12:59.640 --> 01:13:03.812
think there is a series
of examples we have next.

01:13:03.812 --> 01:13:06.270
So these candidates would have
a [INAUDIBLE] bootstrapping.

01:13:06.270 --> 01:13:08.640
So the association of
computing machinery

01:13:08.640 --> 01:13:12.810
has special interest groups
in computer human interaction,

01:13:12.810 --> 01:13:16.870
in groupwear, and the
web is hyper-media stuff.

01:13:16.870 --> 01:13:21.630
So talking those, which each
is an improvement community,

01:13:21.630 --> 01:13:24.250
talking those into
becoming NICs--

01:13:24.250 --> 01:13:26.890
so we've already been knocking
on their doors and saying,

01:13:26.890 --> 01:13:30.030
it's something where they
should get funding for them

01:13:30.030 --> 01:13:33.600
to start turning into NICs
so that they accelerate what

01:13:33.600 --> 01:13:36.650
they're doing and bring
their own capabilities

01:13:36.650 --> 01:13:38.841
in an advanced way
in for every NIC.

01:13:38.841 --> 01:13:40.840
And there are a lot of
things about organization

01:13:40.840 --> 01:13:45.010
and coordination that they
don't foster very well.

01:13:45.010 --> 01:13:48.220
But good solid NICs
LAI can become,

01:13:48.220 --> 01:13:51.270
would be able to contribute a
lot about how you can better

01:13:51.270 --> 01:13:52.410
run and operate those.

01:13:52.410 --> 01:13:56.640
But these guys will produce
much, complete a very lot

01:13:56.640 --> 01:13:58.410
of dialogue and studies.

01:13:58.410 --> 01:14:04.500
And so there are professional
societies on strategic business

01:14:04.500 --> 01:14:07.350
intelligence, on
intellectual capital,

01:14:07.350 --> 01:14:11.200
on scenario development, which
is a very important thing.

01:14:11.200 --> 01:14:13.470
So their whole
pursuit is how do you

01:14:13.470 --> 01:14:15.570
do better scenario development?

01:14:15.570 --> 01:14:18.030
So bringing that
into every NIC would

01:14:18.030 --> 01:14:20.550
be very important for
going into the future.

01:14:23.620 --> 01:14:26.730
And it's clear that security
is an important one inside

01:14:26.730 --> 01:14:31.530
of every organization, and
workflow, electronic commerce.

01:14:31.530 --> 01:14:35.610
And there's no big problem
in going out and finding

01:14:35.610 --> 01:14:39.510
improvement community domains,
that if you could recruit them

01:14:39.510 --> 01:14:42.570
to become NICs as
part of this alliance,

01:14:42.570 --> 01:14:46.050
that their activity would be
very stimulating to every NIC

01:14:46.050 --> 01:14:48.160
and valued.

01:14:48.160 --> 01:14:55.200
So this is the sort of
bootstrapping strategy

01:14:55.200 --> 01:14:57.710
like that, of how to do that.

01:14:57.710 --> 01:15:00.730
And so this sort of
finishes the picture

01:15:00.730 --> 01:15:02.394
about the bootstrapping.

01:15:02.394 --> 01:15:04.060
And there are a few
other things to talk

01:15:04.060 --> 01:15:05.320
about [INAUDIBLE] saying.

01:15:05.320 --> 01:15:07.840
We have quite a few different
organizations that we've

01:15:07.840 --> 01:15:09.550
been involved with talking.

01:15:09.550 --> 01:15:12.160
Some of them are getting
quite active, and some of them

01:15:12.160 --> 01:15:16.120
are fairly passive, which is
the way any consortia, I'm sure,

01:15:16.120 --> 01:15:17.860
starts like this.

01:15:20.460 --> 01:15:24.010
And internationally,
the Japanese chapter

01:15:24.010 --> 01:15:26.740
formed in there, that we're
in the process of forming

01:15:26.740 --> 01:15:29.020
explicitly a US chapter.

01:15:29.020 --> 01:15:30.460
And the current
Bootstrap Alliance

01:15:30.460 --> 01:15:32.110
will be called
the International,

01:15:32.110 --> 01:15:34.990
to try to help
facilitate every nation,

01:15:34.990 --> 01:15:41.770
because every nation has a
distinctive culture in which

01:15:41.770 --> 01:15:45.460
the way in there that you do
the best at collective knowledge

01:15:45.460 --> 01:15:49.630
work would need to evolve
and adapt to the culture.

01:15:49.630 --> 01:15:52.210
And you know the culture's
going to have to shift and adapt

01:15:52.210 --> 01:15:54.520
to make that work.

01:15:54.520 --> 01:15:56.830
And then at the
same time, you have

01:15:56.830 --> 01:15:59.500
to have international
collaboration and cooperation.

01:15:59.500 --> 01:16:03.470
So you have to have underlying
standards that are the same.

01:16:03.470 --> 01:16:05.660
And there's a lot you can
learn from each other.

01:16:05.660 --> 01:16:09.390
So it'll be interesting to see
how the Japanese go after that.

01:16:09.390 --> 01:16:12.430
But one of the early NICs
that they're really pursuing

01:16:12.430 --> 01:16:15.610
is trying to get end
user organizations

01:16:15.610 --> 01:16:18.580
into this business
of how do they

01:16:18.580 --> 01:16:21.970
start pushing proactively,
which is a very important thing.

01:16:25.510 --> 01:16:28.370
So this national
improvement infrastructure,

01:16:28.370 --> 01:16:31.880
it's something that
would be important.

01:16:31.880 --> 01:16:35.920
You could have a whole DOD
improvement infrastructure

01:16:35.920 --> 01:16:38.680
if we wanted to, or an
aerospace improvement

01:16:38.680 --> 01:16:41.650
infrastructure that can go
deeper and broader than you

01:16:41.650 --> 01:16:43.750
guys go.

01:16:43.750 --> 01:16:47.680
And in the DOD, you look
at the planning that

01:16:47.680 --> 01:16:50.110
starts at the strategic
level with the scenarios

01:16:50.110 --> 01:16:54.340
and plans, and drops down into
tactical or actual emergency

01:16:54.340 --> 01:16:56.850
situations, and you
realize that there

01:16:56.850 --> 01:17:00.250
is a lot of concurrency
issue about developing

01:17:00.250 --> 01:17:03.310
that knowledge, and
that in a lot of ways,

01:17:03.310 --> 01:17:06.850
to make that organization
a lot more effective,

01:17:06.850 --> 01:17:08.950
if they try to do it
all by themselves,

01:17:08.950 --> 01:17:10.630
they're going to
miss out on what

01:17:10.630 --> 01:17:13.540
the rest of the world and
the commercial contributions

01:17:13.540 --> 01:17:15.670
and all the other
organizations are doing

01:17:15.670 --> 01:17:18.520
to learn how to make
organizational effectiveness

01:17:18.520 --> 01:17:19.640
a lot higher.

01:17:19.640 --> 01:17:22.060
So it behooves the
Defense Department

01:17:22.060 --> 01:17:23.170
to participate in this.

01:17:23.170 --> 01:17:30.820
So anyway, the sort of
innocent assumption I have is--

01:17:30.820 --> 01:17:35.520
and I've been innocent
all these years--

01:17:35.520 --> 01:17:37.330
that I say, well, look.

01:17:37.330 --> 01:17:40.090
There's something where
there's a real argument that's

01:17:40.090 --> 01:17:43.630
been evolving for years and
is honed and has withstood

01:17:43.630 --> 01:17:47.620
a lot of different sort of
chopping attacks to say, oh,

01:17:47.620 --> 01:17:51.860
you don't know the marketplace
and things like that.

01:17:51.860 --> 01:17:54.440
Anyway, there's something there
that I think needs attention.

01:17:54.440 --> 01:17:57.040
And one of the best
things that happened

01:17:57.040 --> 01:18:02.200
is for us to get actual dialogue
with people like you in there.

01:18:02.200 --> 01:18:06.370
So it would take some
specific commitment in LAI

01:18:06.370 --> 01:18:10.420
to sort of pull out some
people and resources

01:18:10.420 --> 01:18:13.620
to actually participate
in the alliance

01:18:13.620 --> 01:18:16.030
with the other emergent ones.

01:18:16.030 --> 01:18:18.160
It would even be better,
it would also be well,

01:18:18.160 --> 01:18:20.740
if they could go
knock on other doors

01:18:20.740 --> 01:18:22.850
that they know to say, oh.

01:18:22.850 --> 01:18:25.450
This is a bigger scale thing.

01:18:25.450 --> 01:18:33.000
And you'll have to excuse
my sniffling and my hacking.

01:18:33.000 --> 01:18:37.650
I came down with a mixture of
flu and pneumonia a month ago.

01:18:37.650 --> 01:18:41.290
And boy, I have
not recovered yet.

01:18:41.290 --> 01:18:43.110
It's amazing.

01:18:43.110 --> 01:18:46.620
So anyway, this is what I'd
sort of like to leave you with,

01:18:46.620 --> 01:18:48.584
is the-- my god.

01:18:51.510 --> 01:18:55.380
I just, I've got a
set of pet peeves.

01:18:55.380 --> 01:18:59.520
And one of them is that the
Apple Macintosh steadfastly

01:18:59.520 --> 01:19:02.400
only has one button
on the mouse, see?

01:19:02.400 --> 01:19:04.200
And we had three.

01:19:04.200 --> 01:19:07.050
And somebody says how
did you pick three?

01:19:07.050 --> 01:19:10.020
Well, that's only because
for the current status,

01:19:10.020 --> 01:19:11.520
microswitches and
stuff, three are

01:19:11.520 --> 01:19:13.860
all we could get on the mouse.

01:19:13.860 --> 01:19:18.510
And how you can work your hands
better and connect better,

01:19:18.510 --> 01:19:21.378
it's something we just
need to break away from.

01:19:21.378 --> 01:19:25.110
Anyway, that's a substance
of another whole lecture.

01:19:30.080 --> 01:19:34.400
Okay, so there's a whole
open source pursuit.

01:19:34.400 --> 01:19:37.470
And I just have to close rapidly
because, without Earl there,

01:19:37.470 --> 01:19:42.210
I'm trying to be
awkwardly honest about--

01:19:42.210 --> 01:19:46.410
uncharacteristically honest,
whatever-- in closing on time.

01:19:46.410 --> 01:19:48.620
But there's this--

01:19:48.620 --> 01:19:52.500
OHS is something that
we're also trying

01:19:52.500 --> 01:19:55.410
to build communities
that want to participate

01:19:55.410 --> 01:19:56.880
in its evolution.

01:19:56.880 --> 01:20:00.990
And every end user
community like this

01:20:00.990 --> 01:20:04.170
should get involved in
that, in what you're

01:20:04.170 --> 01:20:06.800
going to want to see happen.

01:20:06.800 --> 01:20:10.500
And [INAUDIBLE] Well
hell, open source

01:20:10.500 --> 01:20:14.310
is a really brand new way to
go after complex software.

01:20:14.310 --> 01:20:19.470
And high performance teams are a
really important strategic part

01:20:19.470 --> 01:20:20.760
of all this thing.

01:20:20.760 --> 01:20:23.580
And it doesn't mean you
go to some university

01:20:23.580 --> 01:20:26.970
and say, make a high performance
team that can show how

01:20:26.970 --> 01:20:28.200
dazzling stuff.

01:20:28.200 --> 01:20:32.370
It means recruiting and
equipping and training

01:20:32.370 --> 01:20:36.210
a group of people
for real application

01:20:36.210 --> 01:20:38.560
work inside your organizations.

01:20:38.560 --> 01:20:41.370
And there's some very clear
ways in which, strategically,

01:20:41.370 --> 01:20:44.190
that can be done, and a
very clear way in which that

01:20:44.190 --> 01:20:46.140
would facilitate evolution.

01:20:46.140 --> 01:20:49.890
And that leads, then,
to the sort of say,

01:20:49.890 --> 01:20:55.860
another thing that's necessary
is to have in your tool system,

01:20:55.860 --> 01:20:58.360
have classes of user interface.

01:20:58.360 --> 01:21:00.780
So you could have sort
of the pedestrian class,

01:21:00.780 --> 01:21:03.180
or even the handicapped
class, whether it's

01:21:03.180 --> 01:21:07.950
handicapped this way or visually
or whatever, but also grades

01:21:07.950 --> 01:21:09.870
of higher performance.

01:21:09.870 --> 01:21:12.720
All of it can work over
the same knowledge base.

01:21:12.720 --> 01:21:16.950
And then it gives you the idea
about high performance support

01:21:16.950 --> 01:21:19.080
teams as another whole thing.

01:21:19.080 --> 01:21:21.900
It would take a while to develop
that in some substantial way

01:21:21.900 --> 01:21:22.530
in your minds.

01:21:22.530 --> 01:21:24.770
But that's a really
important thing.

01:21:24.770 --> 01:21:27.570
And a key utilization
for them is

01:21:27.570 --> 01:21:30.790
to support larger conventionally
capable communities,

01:21:30.790 --> 01:21:33.130
organizations or project teams.

01:21:33.130 --> 01:21:35.520
So that's another
whole strategic thing

01:21:35.520 --> 01:21:38.730
that, if that got started,
and introducing those

01:21:38.730 --> 01:21:42.630
into your world would
add an element of--

01:21:42.630 --> 01:21:45.610
it's like those outposts
out in the frontier.

01:21:45.610 --> 01:21:47.990
It would out a very,
very important element

01:21:47.990 --> 01:21:50.400
in a lot of ways to
the way in which,

01:21:50.400 --> 01:21:53.800
not only LAI could evolve,
but your organizations.

01:21:53.800 --> 01:21:57.090
So this multi-class user
interface is very important.

01:21:57.090 --> 01:22:00.080
And our systems have
had that in the past.

01:22:00.080 --> 01:22:02.110
There are many, many things.

01:22:02.110 --> 01:22:04.440
So I think pretty soon I'm
going to come to the end.

01:22:08.330 --> 01:22:12.900
A serious estimate was
made that, in 30 years,

01:22:12.900 --> 01:22:15.960
the world's knowledge will
double every three months.

01:22:15.960 --> 01:22:18.450
And so you say, wow.

01:22:18.450 --> 01:22:19.560
Just think.

01:22:19.560 --> 01:22:24.750
So assumedly, the world's
knowledge about fluid dynamics,

01:22:24.750 --> 01:22:30.010
all sorts of things, the
electronics, the many things

01:22:30.010 --> 01:22:32.560
will change, that are
relevant to your world.

01:22:32.560 --> 01:22:35.460
So how do your particular
knowledge specialists

01:22:35.460 --> 01:22:37.230
evolve to keep up with that?

01:22:37.230 --> 01:22:39.200
What happens to universities?

01:22:39.200 --> 01:22:43.140
Who's going to provide the
education and indoctrination

01:22:43.140 --> 01:22:46.510
that this really big changes?

01:22:46.510 --> 01:22:50.640
So anyway, how do you keep
your knowledge repository

01:22:50.640 --> 01:22:51.810
dynamically updated?

01:22:51.810 --> 01:22:54.060
Is it every three
months or every month

01:22:54.060 --> 01:22:55.950
or every week or every day?

01:22:55.950 --> 01:22:59.340
So it becomes a really,
really critical thing.

01:22:59.340 --> 01:23:02.250
So it's sort of like
a human's reflexes.

01:23:02.250 --> 01:23:04.040
If you're in a complex
environment like,

01:23:04.040 --> 01:23:05.790
say, you've got the
ball and you're trying

01:23:05.790 --> 01:23:08.010
to do open field running.

01:23:08.010 --> 01:23:10.980
Boy, you've got to just pick
up everything right away

01:23:10.980 --> 01:23:13.890
and integrate it into your
new plans and changes.

01:23:13.890 --> 01:23:17.280
Well, more and more of the
world's going to get like that.

01:23:24.450 --> 01:23:27.860
Okay, I did it.

01:23:27.860 --> 01:23:29.455
I'm 42 seconds over.

01:23:29.455 --> 01:23:30.330
MODERATOR: Thank you.

01:23:40.130 --> 01:23:45.292
I'm going to [INAUDIBLE]
took my watch away.

01:23:45.292 --> 01:23:46.500
It seems to work pretty well.

01:23:46.500 --> 01:23:50.289
I know you probably all have
a question or two for Doug,

01:23:50.289 --> 01:23:52.580
but we decided rather than
having a question and answer

01:23:52.580 --> 01:23:56.417
period, we're going to have
a panel later this morning.

01:23:56.417 --> 01:23:58.500
And I would encourage you,
if you have a question,

01:23:58.500 --> 01:23:59.875
just write it on
a piece of paper

01:23:59.875 --> 01:24:01.850
and leave it on the
front table here.

01:24:01.850 --> 01:24:03.440
And Dan [? Ruess ?]
is a fast reader.

01:24:03.440 --> 01:24:05.480
He can process
knowledge quickly.

01:24:05.480 --> 01:24:09.560
And maybe he can figure out how
to incorporate your curiosities

01:24:09.560 --> 01:24:12.620
into the panel.

01:24:12.620 --> 01:24:15.890
I think we timed our break well.

01:24:15.890 --> 01:24:18.160
I feel like I have gotten a--

01:24:18.160 --> 01:24:20.390
there's this
cartoon I have which

01:24:20.390 --> 01:24:23.180
shows this odd kid with kind
of eyes that are coming out

01:24:23.180 --> 01:24:24.400
the side of his head.

01:24:24.400 --> 01:24:26.000
And he raises his hand and
says, "Teacher, teacher.

01:24:26.000 --> 01:24:26.750
Can I be excused?

01:24:26.750 --> 01:24:28.950
My mind is full."

01:24:28.950 --> 01:24:30.120
Let's take a break.

01:24:30.120 --> 01:24:31.550
We're back at 10:30.

01:24:31.550 --> 01:24:33.440
And feel free to
engage Doug one on one

01:24:33.440 --> 01:24:36.280
if you want to pursue
anything further.

